808	3825	801	4	The opening scene on Omaha Beach lives rent-free in my head. Not because I want it to, but because my father — whose special interest is the World Wars — wanted me to "understand the horrors and reality of war" at 12 years old. I'm truly unsure why it couldn't have waited until I was old enough to *actually* go to war, but it is what it is.\r\n\r\nAnyway, I vividly remember a man having his arm blown off, stumbling in shock, and then picking up his own arm. I vividly remember listening to a man screaming for his mother as he laid on the beach with his intestines on the sand beside him. That was about the point where my dad paused the movie because I was sobbing so hard I couldn't see the screen anymore. Not to give me a break; he just wanted to make sure I didn't miss anything.\r\n\r\nSo. The point of me explaining that is so you understand how deeply I mean it when I say that in terms of hammering home the horrors of war, this film easily gets a 10/10 for Omaha Beach. I just wish it wasn't a core memory for me, but here we are.\r\n\r\nThe rest of the film is a lot more tame, but I remember being extremely frustrated watching characters I cared about die for a character I hadn't really yet seen and cared nothing about, for what seemed to me to be a nonsensical reason. Like, congrats, you saved one dude so he would go home to his mom so she wouldn't lose all her children, meanwhile multiple mothers lost children on the way to get that guy. I'm unconvinced the math balances out.\r\n\r\nThat being said, I'm a very character-driven viewer; I can usually forgive a mediocre or even outright bad plot if the characters draw me in and make me care about them. That was true of this movie. Unfortunately. 12-year-old me would have preferred hating them all, but such was not the case. Private Jackson, the sniper, was my favourite. He was the one guy who was like "This is stupid." Immediately endeared me to him.\r\n\r\nOverall, I consider it a good film. Unfortunately, the opening scene at Omaha Beach is its strongest point, and if you're someone who prefers logistics over emotional framing, you're likely to consider the plot needless and foolish drama/sacrifice/tragedy, because there was no need for these men to go on this mission, and therefore the consequences were senseless. But if you prefer emotional framing over logistics, this film will likely resonate with you. Neither viewpoint is better; it's just a matter of perspective.	\N	\N	pending	2026-06-21 05:16:46+03	2026-06-23 01:06:33+03
