1077	8356	18	4	I thought that this had something of the "Lars and the Real Girl" (2007) as the fantasy of a man seeking comfort from that latex version of a partner goes full circle and turns into one with a virtual one without even a stopper to top it up with. "Theodore" (Joaquin Phoenix) is just about divorced from "Catherine" (Rooney Mara), whom he still very much loves, and is struggling with the loneliness of a life that's occasionally spent socialising but mostly spent gaming on his own at home. He sees an ad for a new virtual companion operating system and so into his life comes the self-named "Samantha" (Scarlett Johansson). This isn't just a disjointed voice in the room, though, this is a programme that can grow, can associate and that can develop into something altogether more personal in it's - or perhaps it's best writing "her" - relationship with the owner. The more time they spend in each other's company, the closer they become and the happier the hitherto dejected "Theodore" becomes. Of course, she is a bit more sophisticated than many of his previous digital partners who just wanted him to saucily strangle them with a dead cat's tail, but still there must come a point where an element of physicality is going to be required - else frustration is going to start taking it's toll on their idyllic situation. How can there be any future for the pair? The philosophy behind this doesn't auger very well for the future of our species - though I did quite like the idea of being able to turn a partner on and off at will. Phoenix imbues his character with a degree of amiable mediocrity that is so readily recognisable in many of us, and the gradual dependency he acquires for friends pre-programmed to please and to acquiesce to his every desire sends quite a chilling signal of just how quickly we can lose the ability to co-exist with another free-spirited person with whom compromise and conciliation are constant requirements of day-to-day living. There is a great deal of dialogue here which in it's own way is effective at illustrating just how linear a relationship can be with something/someone that has no other means of communication - even facial expressions or gesturing. "Samantha", being a perfect specimen, also must learn to accommodate the flaws of her new friend - and as the story unfolds and thanks to some sparing interaction with his closest human friend "Amy" (Amy Adams), we learn a lot more about the demons that inhabit the world of a man who doesn't have his emotional problems to seek. What's most terrifying about this is that we can see a worrying degree of plausibility in it all. People whose social interactions are solely based on computer generated personalities designed to make you feel good - but then also why not to manipulate the vulnerable and the depressed, to extort or to wreak psychological havoc? How can anyone possibly broach that void between reality and fantasy and yet still keep hold of their reason and perspective? It mush have been quite tough for Phoenix to spend much of this film talking to himself, or to an extracorporeal voice but he does it engagingly and though I wasn't entirely sure I felt sorry for him in the end, I didn't think him quite the drip I did at the start.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-08 09:10:19+03	2026-08-08 09:15:57+03
