2293	12704	2286	5	Disney's *Encanto* delivers what we expect from the beloved studio-Colorful animation, quirky characters, a respect for the culture it's honoring, powerful themes, and a great soundtrack-while giving the movie a Colombian twist. It might seem at first that the story, which is about Mirabel Madrigal, the 15-year old girl who lives in a family of magical humans, trying to save her family's miracle from dying to prove she is just as capable, has some issues, like with Abuela Alma, the matriarch, being a strict controlling parental figure who is forcing the family to be "perfect" or Mirabel being the only in the family with no powers being a plot hole because Alma, Felix, and Agustin Madrigal all lack powers too, but as you dive deeper into the narrative and watch more carefully, that starts to vanish. Alma's husband, Pedro, died years ago, and so her behavior is a coping mechanism to deal with her excessive PTSD. As for Mirabel, she is specifically the only *child* in the Madrigal family with no powers, as even her youngest cousin, Antonio, gets the gift of talking to animals. The movie has a brisk pace, so it never feels like it's rushing too fast or slowing down too soon, and the light tone allows for a good blend of emotional nuance and lighthearted comedy. Every joke or physical comedic punchline in the movie is a pure delight and makes me laugh every time I watch it. I also really liked how they animated the *Casita* like a person, as it shows that even though it is just a house, it has just as much personality as the people who live in it. The designs of the characters, which appear to be caricatured, also show vibrant expression and plenty of color, ranging from Isabela's pink dress to Mirabel's blue embroidered skirt. What I think really sells the movie, however, is the soundtrack. Lin-Manuel Miranda's signature writing style is on full display here, with every song in the movie being a banger, although my personal favorite has to be "We Don't Talk About Bruno". This song has the perfect combination of upbeat rhythm and dark, moody atmosphere, with the two styles clashing in a way that fits with how the Madrigals perceive Bruno as a character: A quirky, goofy man who unfortunately has led to some bad things happening with his ability to see into people's futures. I honestly cannot recommend this movie enough, as it deserves all the praise it gets. I really hope we get a sequel.	\N	\N	pending	2026-09-14 21:04:05+03	2026-09-14 21:19:43+03
2294	5712	33	4	Each breath you take is a sin. No shadow will be safe again, for you will be hunted by angels.\r\n\r\nThe Da Vinci Code is directed by Ron Howard and adapted to screenplay by Akiva Goldsman from the novel of the same name written by Dan Brown. It stars Tom Hanks, Audrey Tautou, Ian McKellen, Paul Bettany, Jean Reno, Alfred Molina and Jürgen Prochnow. Music is scored by Hans Zimmer and cinematography by Salvatore Totino.\r\n\r\nWhen a man is murdered inside the Louvre, his body is found to be surrounded by cryptic messages. The police call in American symbology expert Robert Langdon (Hanks) to decode the clues. When he is joined by Parisian cryptologist Sophie Neveu (Tautou), it quickly becomes apparent that nothing is as it first seems and a mystery begins to unravel that could shake Christianity to its very core.\r\n\r\nExtended Cut (Blu-ray) Version Viewed.\r\n\r\nI haven't read the book! And I love treasure hunt/clue chasing movies! So I came to Ron Howard's hugely successful film (over $600 million in worldwide profit) bereft of literary pressure and with only a modicum of genre expectation. Perhaps this is why I'm apparently only in a small percentage of film lovers who really enjoyed the film? In spite of those gargantuan financial figures.\r\n\r\nUltimately it's very safe film making, with a director and cast guaranteeing professionalism, but it weaves a magical mystery tour full of cryptic clues, secret organisations and cover ups. Yes, there is a good deal of corn thrown in as well, which inevitably stops the adaptation from being hyper intelligent. There's also an understanding on my part as to why many feel it's just too talky, but was the film ever going to be unfurled as an action movie blockbuster for the popcorn munching crowd, like National Treasure et al?\r\n\r\nIt is a clever conspiracy thriller full of twists and turns with a outlandish revelation at the finale. It's also very appropriately performed by the cast (serio brooding and fret), and when McKellen joins the fray after an hour of film it has a little class as well, while Zimmer's score is a majestic blending of choir, strings and synths. I maintain that the film didn't deserve the critical whacking it got, but again I say I had a blank canvas going in for my first viewing. \r\n\r\nI wasn't sold to it because of controversy or had a saliva tinged mouth having worshipped at the altar of Dan Brown, I expected exactly what I got. A flawed but ever so intriguing adult mystery thriller, and not even Hanks' hair stopped me having a great time with the movie. 7/10	\N	\N	pending	2019-09-02 03:36:15+03	2024-05-16 18:22:24+03
