2583	11031	69	2	**_When the impossible mission becomes implausible overkill_**\r\n\r\nMy first plunge into this popular Tom Cruise franchise was the original 1996 movie, which I saw five years ago. It’s a quality spy/caper flick with the expected convoluted dialogues, espionage gadgets, high society galas, foggy cobblestone streets, sudden deaths, globetrotting, double agents, capers and mind-blowing action. It was just too tortuous for my tastes with not enough human interest, not to mention hampered by the several occasions where a person's fake face is torn off à la Scooby-Doo.\r\n\r\nSo, I jumped ahead 22 years to this 2018 installment since it’s the most popular film in the series, the sixth one, in fact. Once again, the cast and filmmaking are topnotch augmented by lots of action, such as the early pristine restroom bruhaha in Paris. It’s thrilling, sure, but the Asian antagonist effortlessly ripping a pipe from underneath a sink was a bad sign. I could see him doing that if he was a cyborg or drank the super soldier formula but, no, he’s just a regular human being proficient at martial arts.\r\n\r\nThen there’s the wild melee at a posh nightclub involving The White Widow (Vanessa Kirby) wherein the participants nonchalantly discuss business in the backroom 2 minutes afterward without a bead of sweat and none the worse for wear. Why Sure! Do I need to go into how the team miraculously defuses three nuclear bombs in the climax?\r\n\r\nObviously the creators have upped the ante in each film to the point where the goings-on can only be described as complete detachment from reality. It’s like watching a live-action Road Runner cartoon for 2½ hours. Um, no thanks.\r\n\r\nGRADE: C-/D+	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-03 05:57:04+03	2026-07-03 05:57:04+03
