3240	9918	3233	5	Robert Eggers’ *The Witch* does something rather nastier than simply putting a witch in the woods. It builds a family perfectly capable of destroying itself, then puts the Devil close enough to offer assistance.\r\n\r\nWilliam takes his family out of their settlement in pursuit of religious purity and promptly creates a tiny kingdom governed by his own pride. The crops fail, money disappears, truths are withheld and Thomasin occupies the particularly unenviable position of being old enough to work, old enough to blame, but not old enough to possess any meaningful authority. By the time anyone seriously accuses her of witchcraft, the family has already constructed the machinery required to believe it.\r\n\r\nEggers makes that machinery horribly convincing. Samuel vanishes during a game of peekaboo with an impossibility that the film barely underlines. There is open ground everywhere except towards the forest, so Thomasin walks towards the forest. What else can she do? That refusal to overplay the impossible becomes one of the film’s great strengths. Even the supernatural arrives with unnerving discretion, right down to the almost subliminal progression from Black Phillip’s hoof to the boot of something considerably less goat-shaped.\r\n\r\nMeanwhile, the human details hurt more. Katherine climbing into Caleb’s grave beside him is seen at a distance, a tiny action inside a huge landscape, and says more about her grief than another page of Puritan anguish could manage. Caleb’s extraordinary death scene and the shrieking twins around him make faith itself feel contagious.\r\n\r\nThere is an interesting argument that *The Witch* need not even be supernatural: bad crops, religious mania and a story subsequently told as New England folklore could explain plenty. The film rather spoils that tidy escape route by showing us the witch when nobody is there to hallucinate her. Quite right too. The richer idea is that the witches are real **and** this family has made itself exquisitely vulnerable to them.\r\n\r\nThat is why Thomasin’s ending works. She has not secretly been the witch all along. She becomes one. Her approach to the coven is hesitant, almost appalled, until she rises with the others and something finally changes in her face.\r\n\r\nAfter ninety minutes of obedience, accusation and misery, living deliciously does have quite the sales pitch.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nReviewed by FolknHell, the folk horror film podcast · folknhell com\r\n\r\nHear the full FolknHell podcast discussion of The Witch: folknhell com/the-witch-review along with our other reviews, transcripts and episodes.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-26 00:59:48+03	2026-08-26 00:59:48+03
