4239	249	69	5	**_Highly entertaining, but also... enlightening_**\r\n\r\nA tornado rips through Dorothy's home in Kansas and transports her to the land of Oz where she meets a scarecrow in need of a brain, a tin man in need of a heart and a lion in need of courage. So they venture to the Emerald City in search of the eponymous wizard. \r\n\r\n"The Wizard of Oz" (1939) is eminently entertaining for both kids and adults. The message is great to boot. Yes, it's about developing your head, heart, and courage to find your way "home," but its most potent moral is about pulling back the curtain on political authorities and religion to expose the feeble phony pulling the levers.\r\n\r\nWhich isn't to say that all such authorities are phonies, of course, just that we need to be ever conscious of the fakes trying to manipulate us by pulling our strings.\r\n\r\nThe creators were smart to actually make the wicked witch of the west, her castle and flying monkeys creepy and horrific (in a comic booky way). \r\n\r\nThe film runs 1 hour, 42 minutes.\r\n\r\nGRADE: A+	\N	\N	pending	2023-07-25 21:13:05+03	2023-07-25 21:13:05+03
4240	249	1315	5	When we revisit a classic, it's hard not to let nostalgia get in the way. But rewatching the most famous trip down the yellow brick road proves that the movie isn't held back by the technical limits of its time. Instead, it still has an incredible energy and charm that makes it truly timeless.\r\n\r\nJudy Garland is the absolute heart of the film. Her genuine vulnerability and very human longing pull us right into the fantasy, acting as the perfect bridge between dry, dusty Kansas and the dazzling world of Oz. The supporting cast is just as brilliant. Ray Bolger’s Scarecrow is a masterclass in physical comedy; his fluid, clumsy movements and amazing makeup make him the emotional compass of the group. Jack Haley brings a touching warmth to the Tin Man, despite being stuck in a rigid, heavy silver suit, making his search for a heart feel incredibly real. And Bert Lahr completely steals the show as the Cowardly Lion. Wearing a brutally heavy costume made of real lion skin, he fully embraces the ridiculousness of his character, turning his exaggerated fear into comedy gold.\r\n\r\nThe movie's technical achievements are still mind-blowing today. That iconic shift from sepia-toned Kansas to the bright Technicolor of Oz wasn't just a cheap gimmick; the bold colors perfectly captured the feeling of stepping into a dream. You can also really feel the craftsmanship in the practical effects and massive hand-painted MGM sets. Unlike modern CGI, things like the terrifying studio-made tornado and the creepy Flying Monkeys have a physical weight and honesty to them that is hard to replicate. The songs do the same heavy lifting. Classics like "Over the Rainbow" aren't just random musical breaks to show off vocal talent; they actually drive the story forward and capture complex emotions in a beautifully simple way.\r\n\r\nThe film does have a few flaws, though. It loses a little steam in the middle because the second act gets pretty repetitive—Dorothy walks, meets someone, talks, faces a small threat, and repeats. But the biggest letdown is the "it was all a dream" ending. It feels like a frustrating cop-out that completely erases all the character growth, real fears, and sacrifices the group went through. Even worse, it leaves a pretty conformist message, basically telling a young, ambitious girl to stop exploring and just settle for a boring life at home.\r\n\r\nEven with those pacing and story issues, the movie's legacy is undeniable. It was a massive gamble that set the gold standard for Hollywood filmmaking. But beyond the groundbreaking sets and makeup, the movie survives because its core themes—searching for courage, love, and a place to belong—are universal. If you watch it today, try to look past the childhood nostalgia and pop culture hype. You’ll find a movie made by a giant studio machine that somehow managed to capture a very real, authentic heart.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-13 05:57:15+03	2026-08-13 05:57:15+03
