4363	22	4356	5	Writer/director Curry Barker’s leap from YouTube viral fame to the big screen is one of the best success stories in Hollywood, and Kane Parsons’ **BACKROOMS** for A24 later this month appears to be further proof that the road from YouTube to Hollywood — while still less traveled — can make all the difference.\r\n\r\nThe viral success of Barker's [**MILK & SERIAL** (2024)](https://letterboxd.com/fixateandbinge/film/milk-serial/) showcased enormous potential and a natural instinct for low-budget horror filmmaking, making his transition to the big screen feel like a well-earned graduation.\r\n\r\nIn **OBSESSION**, Barker has crafted a slow-burn psychological horror film that steadily builds careens towards its shocking conclusion.\r\n\r\nWhile the deliberate pacing occasionally tested my patience, once Barker’s story fully unfolds, it seeps into the viewer’s psyche in ways few recent horror films have managed — provoking deeply uncomfortable questions about consent and free agency, while possibly making you think twice before blowing out candles on your next birthday.\r\n\r\n**OBSESSION** presents a seemingly innocuous setup centered on the lovelorn Bear (Michael Johnston), who literally wishes to escape the friend zone with his longtime pal Nikki (Inde Navarrette). After stumbling upon a “One Wish Willow” collector’s item in a new-age curio shop — one that wouldn’t feel out of place beside the Zoltar machine in the Tom Hanks smash hit BIG (1988) — Bear makes a wish without understanding the terrifying power he has unleashed.\r\n\r\nEncouraged by his best friend Ian (frequent Barker collaborator Cooper Tomlinson), Bear initially appears to get everything he wants when Nikki suddenly develops a romantic interest in him.\r\n\r\nBut soon the wheels start to come off as Bear's happily-ever-after spirals into a nightmare after the possessiveness of Nikki becomes increasingly unhinged. Navarrette delivers a terrifying performance that has echoes of **FATAL ATTRACTION** and **SINGLE, WHITE, FEMALE** -- with additional layers of psychological torture for her loss of bodily autonomy\r\n\r\nWith shocking moments of terror, expertly sustained tension, disturbing imagery, and a fearless performance from Navarrette, **OBSESSION** stands as one of the year’s best horror films.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-29 19:53:09+03	2026-07-29 21:02:04+03
4364	22	1315	4	There’s a fine line between genuine affection and possession, and horror movies usually thrive when crossing it. I went into *Obsession* expecting a psychological deep dive into emotional dependency, but what we actually get is an experience that violently swings between brilliance and frustration. Directed by Curry Barker, the film swings big in its attempt to show toxic masculinity and the loss of free will. The first thirty minutes are genuinely suffocating, doing a great job of making you feel trapped. But while Barker takes huge risks for his debut feature, the movie stumbles trying to keep that momentum going.\r\n\r\nThe real trouble starts when the story needs to stretch past its initial premise. The second act drags hard, trapping the viewer in a repetitive loop of jealousy and chasing that goes nowhere. They could have easily chopped at least twenty minutes in the editing room. On top of that, the indie budget really shows; scenes meant to be psychologically oppressive are ruined by amateurish lighting and set designs that break the immersion. Worse still is the jarring mix of tones. Right when the discomfort peaks, the script throws in bizarre dark humor. A badly timed joke or a goofy reaction completely kills the dramatic weight, making it clear the director hasn't quite learned how to trim the fat from his own ideas.\r\n\r\nThe whole plot with the mystical artifact hooks you at first, but it loses steam once the curse actually kicks in. Instead of building tension, the narrative just stalls. This makes the interactions suffer, too. Dedicated actors are stuck delivering stiff, unnatural dialogue, forced to spell out feelings that their body language was already showing perfectly. It makes some of the more dramatic scenes feel like a bad teen soap opera draft.\r\n\r\nFollowing the protagonist, Bear (Michael Johnston), for almost two hours is a tough pill to swallow. He’s a pathetic guy whose harmless neediness quickly turns into a sick, one-dimensional selfishness. While the movie doesn't shy away from exposing the ugliest sides of possessiveness disguised as romance, Bear has zero redeeming qualities, making the watch tedious since there's no one to root for. If the movie survives its structural flaws, it’s almost entirely thanks to Inde Navarrette. Tasked with playing a character who gets psychologically beaten down by both the male lead and bad writing, she delivers an incredibly mature, visceral performance. Her subtle shifts in posture and gaze anchor the whole movie, making her the only real emotional core in the story.\r\n\r\nUsing folk horror to talk about toxic masculinity is the coolest idea here, but the execution completely lacks subtlety. The movie talks down to the audience, hammering its message home so hard that it feels more like a lecture than an organic psychological study. This also exposes a major flaw in how it handles its female lead. In trying to call out abuse, the narrative reduces her to a mere tool for suffering, tortured just to push the bad guy's arc forward. It leaves you wondering if it's genuinely critiquing misogyny or just exploiting women's trauma for shock value.\r\n\r\nThe film's early strength is its grounded tension, relying on everyday paranoia rather than monsters in the dark. But by the third act, the director throws all that out the window and goes full grotesque. Instead of feeling earned, this sudden bloodbath of graphic violence feels like a desperate crutch to shock an already bored audience. The sound design also fumbles; they went for an eerie lack of music, which works at first, but ends up making long scenes feel empty. When loud noises do happen, they're poorly mixed and feel like cheap jump scares.\r\n\r\nAfter racing through a chaotic, blood-soaked climax, the movie just ends. An abrupt cut to black rolls the credits without explaining the artifact or the rules of the curse. It doesn't feel like a thought-provoking open ending; it just feels like the writers painted themselves into a corner and didn't know how to wrap things up, leaving you waiting for a payoff that never comes.\r\n\r\nIn the end, *Obsession* is a tough, uneven watch. Curry Barker has the guts to poke at the painful wounds of toxic relationships, and Inde Navarrette’s amazing acting keeps things afloat. But the pacing issues, weird tonal shifts, and the rushed, gory climax really test your patience. Still, it forces you to think. Horror is supposed to push us out of our comfort zone, right? If you want to feel that oppressive atmosphere, it's worth a watch. Just go in with a strong stomach, ready for the messy pacing, and see how you handle the sudden silence when those credits finally roll.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-31 04:43:10+03	2026-07-31 04:43:10+03
4365	22	1655	4	**Emotional Hijacking**\r\n\r\nBear is a shy, introverted young man working in a music shop who is deeply and secretly in love with his friend and colleague, Nikki. Desperate over his unrequited romantic longing, Bear acquires a mysterious supernatural object called "The Weeping Willow," which has the power to grant wishes. Blind to the consequences, he wishes for Nikki to fall in love with him "more than anything else in this world." The spell works instantly, but the idyllic dream swiftly morphs into a nightmare: Nikki’s implanted love mutates into an irrational, suffocating, and deeply disturbing behavior. Her morbid devotion erases the boundaries of her sanity, unleashing a downward spiral of violence and obsessive possessiveness that threatens to destroy both their lives and everyone around them.\r\n\r\nIn certain aspects, "Obsession" stands out as the great revelation of contemporary horror cinema. Operating on a shoestring budget of just $750,000, director Curry Barker achieves what many Hollywood blockbusters can only dream of: building an atmosphere of tension relying strictly on the power of ideas, a fresh approach to staging, and raw storytelling.\r\n\r\nBarker taps into 1980s cinematic references, taking the classic premise of magical manipulation (W.W. Jacobs' "The Monkey's Paw") and transplanting it into deeply uncomfortable psychological territory. The horror becomes visceral, psychological, and existential, confronting us with the terrifying facets of the human ego. It seeks to prove that forcing someone to love you does not stem from devotion, but rather from a craving for control and a pathological fear of loneliness or rejection.\r\n\r\nFinally, a humble piece of advice for a talented Curry Barker, who will never read this review but is still in the budding stages of his career: the overreliance on jump-scare audio stingers is symptomatic of an endemic disease—one that blends narrative laziness, behavioral psychology, and the familiar commercial pressures of Hollywood. Do not fall into the temptation of mimicking the cheapest tropes of late-career Shyamalan. Instead, fully exploit that aesthetic and cinematic creativity currently flourishing within you. Flee once and for all from cheap, gimmicky chords, and lose your fear of silence. Remember that true horror dwells in the stillness of masterpieces like "Nosferatu", "Les Diaboliques" or "Rosemary's Baby", by Murnau, Clouzot and Polanski, respectively.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-19 20:44:40+03	2026-08-19 20:44:40+03
4366	22	4359	4	### My little food critic 🗒️\r\n\r\nJust because Current Joys finally got justice for their underrated music doesn’t mean anyone else in this flick did. Long story short: Bear is the true villain of this story. I appreciated Curry Baker’s rom-com subversion. There were moments when Bear seemed like a guy trying to navigate romance in the age of disengagement and misunderstandings. However, at every twist and turn, he failed to correct his mistake. Ultimately dragging the cast of characters to a Shakespearean tragic ending.\r\n\r\nDespite Baker not giving us insight into Nikki’s headspace, I assume she’s in a “Get Out”-adjacent sunken place. As a result of Bear’s haphazard wish, Nikki has become a passive viewer of her relationship with Bear. Frozen at her doorstep, an [obsessive](https://youtu.be/GrNt7Vz68oY?si=x1CsxLA6K-vGQt6X) (take a shot) spirit takes control of her body. Rather than connecting the dots, Bear is blissfully ignorant of the effects of his one wish.\r\n\r\nI haven’t watched the few commentary videos explaining why he’s the villain in an effort to avoid unwarranted spoilers before watching this in theaters. Now that I’ve had my experience with “Obsession,” I understand why the “he’s the villain” phrase has come up numerous times. Bear expected Nikki to be his eager companion who lived predominantly in the heart of his home. However, his inner darkness is alluded to through the color grading and lighting. Taking a Coppola approach to lighting, shadows consume the rooms Bear and Nikki occupy. Naturally, the low lighting heightens our paranoia when Nikki hides in corners, wailing for Bear.\r\n\r\nAs the curse worsens, Bear refuses to acknowledge the severity of his situation. The boundaries he establishes with Nikki are slaps on the wrist, at best. His delusion clouded his judgment so intensely that he doesn’t recognize that he can’t reason with someone in this state of mind. This contradicts his initial concerns from the exposition. His concerns about denying Nikki access to consent and being fearful that she was inebriated all withered away the moment he confessed his feelings for her. He is so careless with the *actual* Nikki, so much so that he waits until the end of Act Two to get curious about how to reverse or contradict the One Wish Willow.\r\n\r\nSecondly, Bear tells Nikki over dinner that he wants to become a food critic one day. Granted, these characters are young people who have a right to evolve and shift their interests, but why do we have ZERO clues about this hidden dream of Bear’s? The main set is his home. Mind you, there are no cookbooks, spice racks, or even elaborate cooking dishes. I interpret this as an intentional production design choice to reveal Bear’s disingenuous passion. Bear claims he’s in love with Nikki, but he never wanted her autonomy or actual demeanor. He wanted to be the center of her world without her input or personality. He wants to be a food critic, yet he couldn’t smell that Sandy-wich?\r\n\r\nBear’s dismal commitment to anything smothered the fire of the women in his life. Nikki had plans of moving out to pursue her writing career. Sarah waited anxiously for an acceptance letter from art school, hoping to be a tattoo artist. All this dissolved into nothingness so Bear could have Sarah and Nikki's enamored attention. (I’m convinced Bear relished knowing Sarah had a crush on him. This boosted his ego, especially since Nikki’s in love with him more than anyone else in the f#%king world.)\r\n\r\nConsidering the amount of passion I have about these characters and how invested I’ve become in their worlds, it’s safe to say I enjoyed this story. The themes about consent and free will were great, and the stakes rose at a decent rate. The pacing was a little off at times. The cinematography really outshone any other choices in this film. The costuming had a small-town alternative vibe we don’t see enough of in mainstream films. Dressing Nikki in red frequently throughout the story was a nice element as well. Inde Navarrette made this film beyond memorable. Her performance control is something to be marveled at. She embodied the mannerisms of a cat while simultaneously distinguishing the wavering boundaries of the real Nikki and the possessed spirit.\r\n\r\nEven though the aesthetics of “Obsession” were well-rounded on screen, this film reminded me of too many other recent horror films to be immersive. The fact that Nikki was basically in the sunken place was a solid homage to “Get Out” (2017), but when Nikki started to say, “no, no, no, no,...” it felt too similar to that kitchen scene for me to feel the energy shift completely. Most of the locations reminded me of different movies (the house = Barbarian (2022), the party scene = Talk to Me (2022)), and the tone was a little too clean to make its voice unique.\r\n\r\nAll in all, I give this film 3.9 stars out of 5 stars. And HEY! We have a new scream queen ❤️‍🔥 \r\n\r\n*Enjoyed this review? Subscribe to my Substack (Snowy Reviews, by Yasmin) and give me a follow (@yasminthewriter) on Letterboxd, and see what I review next!*	\N	\N	pending	2026-09-25 18:18:45+03	2026-09-25 21:16:17+03
4367	6085	4360	4	Following a devastating storm, a small town in Maine finds that the eerie mist swarming into the neighborhood holds a deadly secret inside that attacks anyone around them and forces a small band of survivors trapped inside a supermarket to hold off the creatures.\r\n\r\nThis one here is an incredibly frustrating King work. A lot of what makes this one so uneven is due to there being a lot to really like about it, a few points to love about it and then there were elements to utterly loathe about it. The film is at it's best with the way the mounting hysteria over the situation with them trapped within the supermarket and how the groups resolve their problems and start new ones which was pretty good and makes for a realistic feeling to the story, detailing how it all breaks down and what the group resorts to in order to keep their sanity. Likewise, that in turn leads into the films' best efforts in the first few attacks with the unknown creatures inside the mist killing off the victims as they refuse to believe what's out there and rush foolishly into the area which makes for a series of fun times out in the cargo hold and the different attempts to reach safety outside and then encountering the different creatures and their powers were all good points. The flying insects and them trying to keep them out as the different creatures break in resulting in a series of great encounters trying to clear them out of the store, and the insane battle in the pharmacy during the pitch-black interior is a rather stark and creepy experience fighting off the array of creatures that gives this one a ton of impressive points. The final few battles and escapes to get out, the blood and gore and the way that the creatures are dealt with are all loved points here as that all leads into the stellar finale which is an absolutely unforeseen and shocking in what goes on as there are some rather disturbing parts in this section. Along with the fine creature effects, these here are the good parts to really love about it although it does have a few issues to be had here. The film's biggest problem is the mere appearance of the religious babble that gets spewed in here which is so wrongheaded and rather off-putting it just makes the viewer feel like not watching it all that often. Yet another big issue here is the length and how overlong it feels, with the rather overlong sections in the middle really making it feel it's length somewhat. While there's plenty to like here, these do hold it back.\r\n\r\nRated R: Graphic Language, Graphic Violence and intense themes of children-in-danger.	\N	\N	pending	2017-03-12 05:41:58+03	2021-06-23 18:57:55+03
4368	6085	33	4	You can't convince some people there's a fire even when their hair is burning. Denial is a powerful thing. \r\n\r\nThe Mist is directed by Frank Darabont and Darabont adapts the screenplay from the story of the same name written by Stephen King. It stars Thomas Jane, Marcia Gay Harden, Toby Jones, Laurie Holden and Andre Braugher. Music is by Mark Isham and cinematography by Rohn Schmidt.​\r\n​\r\nResidents of a small Maine town become trapped in the local supermarket when an otherworldly mist brings deadly creatures in full attack mode. That's not the only problem, for two groups form inside the market, one in favour of escaping, the other for expiation.\r\n\r\nAs is the norm, King adaptations vary in quality and divisive fan appraisals, so with "The Mist" on screen not exactly setting the box office alight, and it even getting delayed releases in Europe, one would think this is one of the lesser King adapted lights? Not so, in fact, it is now proving to have a longevity of worth in horror fan circles. So much so, that the great horror writer at the literary source gives it the full thumbs up whilst giving the "changed" ending his full pat on the back approval.\r\n\r\nDarabont is of course the director who previously took King's more human interest stories and crafted much beloved movies out of them, so why was he in the chair to direct a film about alien creatures unleashing bloody dread on small town Americana? Well actually the answer is why "The Mist" is such a cunning chilly delight. For this not only features monstrous creatures straight out of a Lovecraft/Barker nightmare, but also the monsters of the human kind, where the venality of the human condition is laid bare under duress, of which it is very frightening.\r\n\r\nThe alien creatures themselves creep the flesh, ok the effects work sometimes sags under the scrutiny of "HD" viewings, but this is nightmarish stuff, none more so than with a quite brilliant and terrifying sequence of events in a pharmacy. Yet it's the human monsters within the supermarket that usurps our creature invaders, where religious fervour and mans propensity for survival comes crashing together for dynamic results - the cast utterly in tune with the material and delivering quality portrayals.\r\n\r\nOnce the human battles within dissipates, and we come to the resolutions and reasonings of what has caused the creature invasion (hello subtext), we arrive at the much talked about finale. It actually deserves to be divisive, for we don't want yet another horror film finale that has people shrugging their respective shoulders and saying "fair enough, but is that it?".\r\n\r\nIf you buy into the all round bleak tonalities that the pic has been serving throughout, then this ending hits all the right buttons. For sure, this is no easy cop out to send you home with a smile on your face, it's brutal, and crucially it's befitting the word of the genre it sits in - horror. 8.5/10	\N	\N	pending	2019-10-06 05:49:39+03	2024-05-16 18:22:25+03
4369	6085	4362	5	The more I watch this movie, the more convinced I become that this movie is _less_ about the mist as a horrific result of military experiments gone awry, and _more_ a commentary on the fear of death and the unknown beyond.\r\n\r\nThe mist and its Lovecraftianesque creatures are symbolic of humanity's existential dread and the abject fear that there is quite possibly nothing else but this, and the end here is the end forever; or even worse, that what lies beyond is not a benevolent Creator, but rather an entity that either is indifferent to our existence and our plights, or is actively malevolent against us. Jean-Paul Sartre described this fear as "existential nausea."\r\n\r\nMrs. Carmody's ever growing religious cult was the very opposite extreme. She proclaimed to be a prophet of the one true God, yet was hypocritical, judgmental and quick to cast stones even in light of her own sins. Her fundamentalist extremism insured they _never_ escaped their box. But what seemed the logical end result of her many violent transgressions only served to martyr her in the eyes of other extremists -- as it so often does -- and serves, in their twisted minds, as vindication of what they've been preaching all along.\r\n\r\nAnd there are those who simply did the best they could with the circumstances they were given, but in the process, did not sacrifice the wills of others for their own gain while simultaneously giving of themselves unto the service of others. No one can say with absolute certainty what lies in wait beyond this life, but we shouldn't squander the moments we _do_ have on this Earth entombed in fear and letting that fear manipulate us into becoming the monsters we sometimes create ourselves.\r\n\r\nThe end of this movie has always been, in my opinion, the most tragic. The ending was _almost_ a happy one, if only they'd held out for a few moments longer. But in relation to the underlying theme laid out above, this group of people's break from the store to the car to the literal end of the road was metaphorical of venturing out into the unknown, even if it means leaving the comfort yet unstable illusion of safety and well-being, to take our chances navigating life the best we know how. We may feel as if ultimately the only thing we have any control over is when and how we die, but even this is just another illusion.	\N	\N	pending	2020-11-12 09:12:18+03	2021-06-23 18:58:47+03
