5751	25	4356	3	Even Shakespeare's Polonius complained to Prince Hamlet that the traveling actors' Greek Tragedy speeches were too long -- and over four centuries later there is still something to be said about testing audience patience.\r\n\r\nCumulatively, audiences have had to sit through over 9-hours on James Cameron's Pandora in his **AVATAR** films -- with its mind-blowing special effects about the indigenous Na'vi trying to preserve their way of life from intergalactic colonialism hellbent on exploiting their resources.\r\n\r\nThis isn't quite as brutal as Leopold II's actual late 19th century subjugation of the Congolese during the Rubber Trade -- but the environmental messaging was made crystal clear in Cameron's not-so-subtle first film that grossed more than $2 billion at the box office.\r\n\r\n2022's **WAY OF WATER** appropriated Maori culture and Pacific Islander teenage slang with its "cuz" and "bro" -- terms of endearment that Cameron expected his audience to accept as actual common Na'avi sobriquets. I didn't buy it in 2022 -- finding the dialogue cloying, and grating.\r\n\r\nStill, this is James 'Spectacle' Cameron -- and he's nothing if not a talented director of action set pieces, and stunning visuals. In **AVATAR: FIRE AND ASH** -- (which I was able to be immersed in in IMAX 3D) the story delves deeper into the children of Jake Sully (Sam Worthington) and Neytiri (Zoe Saldana) after the events of **WAY OF WATER** weigh heavily on the theme of family, unity and survival.\r\n\r\nBut those are also the same themes from the second film -- and herein lies the central problem of **FIRE AND ASH** -- as it begins to feel like the same dish served with a different garnish.\r\n\r\nIn this overlong second sequel, the same concerns about a resurgent human military targeting the pristine air, lands and seas remain in place -- and the same rogues gallery of bad guys are back for a third helping of hunting down Worthington and Saldana's brood -- led by Stephen Lang's Colonel Miles Quaritch who, like a bad penny, keeps turning up to wreck havoc on Pandora.\r\n\r\nBut Cameron sets aside extolling the virtues of the Noble Savage, introducing audiences to a corruptible and equally warlike Na'vi Ash People, led by Varang (Oona Chaplin) who cavorts around the film smirking and engaging in snarling standoffs with Saldana wearing a distinctive frilled headdress that conjures up memories of Wayne Knight being ambushed in **JURASSIC PARK**.\r\n\r\nWhen Chaplin's Varang inevitably teams up with Lang's Quaritch to face off, again, with Worthington and Saldana -- we're given incredible action set pieces -- that is heavy on style, but lacking, as ever, in substance.\r\n\r\nI didn't hate this film -- it's incredible what Cameron can do with a budget that rivals the GDP of a small Third World country -- but the rendering of the supporting Na'vi characters, as it did in **WAY OF THE WATER**, seemed less-emotive than those of the main Na'vi mains, leaving some of the interactions blank in expressions and testing my ability to differentiate them from other Na'vi characters.\r\n\r\nI wonder how much code is applied to the supporting cast for those computer rendered facial expressions -- and I'm willing to bet they're a lot less than what they give Saldana, Lang and Worthington.\r\n\r\nThese **AVATAR** films have been visual triumphs -- but in the age of AI screensavers and digital images that increasingly test our ability to detect what is real and what isn't -- the novelty that Cameron has enjoyed as a visionary director may be less and less spectacular and awe-inspiring.\r\n\r\n(It's an ironic development in entertainment given that Cameron's **TERMINATOR** films -- especially **T2** -- warned society about the dangers of artificial intelligence).\r\n\r\nDon't get me wrong -- I really love Cameron's earlier work and would like to see him tackle other stories in his enormously imaginative mind -- including a **TRUE LIES** sequel, another **ALITA** film and beyond.\r\n\r\nI see no great tragedy if **AVATAR: FIRE AND ASH** is his final trip to Pandora (notwithstanding there are two more sequels already written, waiting in the wings, presumably on the Box Office returns from this one) -- and handing these off to someone else, say, Guillermo Del Toro might breathe new life into these films the way Irvin Kershner did for the **STAR WARS** franchise with **EMPIRE STRIKES BACK**.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-30 01:19:33+03	2026-07-30 01:19:33+03
