5939	14414	4356	5	No matter how you approach Paul Thomas Anderson's **ONE BATTLE AFTER ANOTHER** whether broadly or myopically, the film has many stories to tell that expand or contract however we view it.\r\n\r\nOn a larger scale, the film is about the oppressive strictures governing society that turn whole swaths of the downtrodden into a community of masses huddled together -- and the revolutionary agents who seek to overthrow these draconian bulwarks.\r\n\r\nOn a smaller scale, the film is also about the bonds between a father and a daughter and the turbulent past that inevitably superimposes itself on the present, threatening to tear asunder those very powerful bonds.\r\n\r\nFilmed in VistaVision, the second film in the 21st century since last year's acclaimed **THE BRUTALIST** -- PTA's film, much like the story, is epic in its larger themes and equally poignant in its more intimate interactions. VistaVision particularly excels with panorama as well as close-ups, two tonally valuable facets of filmmaking that soar in **ONE BATTLE AFTER ANOTHER**.\r\n\r\nTwinned with PTA's frequent musical collaborator, Radiohead's Jonny Greenwood -- their sixth collaboration that is on track to match that of Spielberg and John Williams -- the percussive, jangly score carries us along with adrenaline-charged intensity and occasional rise and fall intonations that embrace the anarchy of the film itself.\r\n\r\nLighter moments, even humorous ones, are perfectly balanced by Greenwood's score and it's long past time for the Academy to bestow its honors or further risk artistic obsolescence.\r\n\r\nPTA's film begins in the chaos of homegrown revolutionaries -- the French 75 -- a far-left, revolutionary group who are actively liberating immigrants in detainment camps along the California/Mexico border, targeting banks and military border security agents in the name of establishing a counterculture new order in an America increasingly controlled by Conservative Nationalists.\r\n\r\nAdapted from Thomas Pynchon's 1990 novel ***'Vineland'*** -- a postmodern novel set against the backdrop of California in 1984 during Ronald Reagan's re-election -- PTA's **ONE BATTLE AFTER ANOTHER** offers a very close analogue to Pynchon's novel, with Leonardo DiCaprio playing fellow revolutionary saboteur leader Pat Calhoun/Bob Ferguson, a bomb specialist alongside Perfidia Beverly Hills -- played by Teyana Taylor.\r\n\r\nIt is is their revolutionary pairing that results in the birth of a daughter, that puts their relationship at the crossroads. Pursued by the aptly named Col. Steven J. Lockjaw, portrayed by Sean Penn in what must be an award-winning performance, soon the French 75 are disbanded after key members of the group are captured.\r\n\r\nSixteen years later, and living under an assumed name, DiCaprio's "Bob" (whose revolutionary days seem long behind him, lost in a haze of bong resin and brunchtime shots of Scotch) is single-father to Willa Ferguson, the incredible film debut of Chase Infiniti, living almost off-grid in northern California.\r\n\r\nWhen the relentless Col. Lockjaw once again is back in pursuit of the disbanded members of the French 75 -- DiCaprio's Bob and Willa find themselves barely able to keep one foot ahead of the long arm of the law.\r\n\r\nRelying on a network of committed locals, memorably from Willa's own Taekwondo Instructor, the scene-stealing Benicio Del Toro as Sensei Sergio St. Carlos who is revealed to be a major figure in the underground railroad movement for undocumented immigrants, the latter half of the film becomes a thrilling pursuit that culminates in one of the most cinematic endings seen.\r\n\r\nThere is so much to enjoy in PTA's **ONE BATTLE AFTER ANOTHER**, with performances from DiCaprio, Infiniti, Taylor, Regina Hall, Del Toro, and especially Penn that are more than enough to hold our attention for the 2 hours and 45 minute runtime that seemingly flies by on the wings of pure cinema.\r\n\r\nIs it PTA's all-time masterpiece? I'll hold my tongue on that one -- but it's surely in the conversation.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-30 01:33:09+03	2026-07-30 01:33:09+03
