6281	14407	4356	5	If you'll indulge me, I am going to offer my very own interpretation of the larger themes that Zach Creggers is going for in **WEAPONS** that left me dumbstruck. I still can't stop thinking about it.\r\n\r\nFirst, we know from the trailer the inciting incident rests on the inexplicable disappearance of 17 school children, curiously all from the same classroom -- at the near-witching hour of 2:17 AM, as the chilling CCTV footage that will surely drive up purchases of Ring doorbell cameras -- that remains unsolved when our film begins.\r\n\r\nCreggers' film shows us the 'how' but not necessarily the 'why' -- which is what I want to discuss here. Spoilers aplenty from here on out, obviously:\r\n\r\nWhy it is called **WEAPONS** eventually becomes clear enough before the credits roll, but I couldn't help think that what the film was really trying to show us was an awesome and terrifying demonstration of power. Raw, uninhibited, pure power. All-consuming, all-encompassing power that someone can have other others.\r\n\r\nAnd it is absolutely chilling.\r\n\r\nNow I am not certain what specific interpretation Creggers is going for -- and I'd quite like for him to adopt the David Lynch penchant for refusing to explicate the means of his films, even if I still think his **BARBARIANS** (2022) is trying to say something (albeit, obliquely) about gentrification.\r\n\r\nSo, for what it's worth, I think **WEAPONS** is a warning about the mental sublimation of radical ideologies in a so-called civil society and how the ability to formulate rational arguments tethered to facts -- is easily discarded in favor of groupthink.\r\n\r\nMore on the nose, I think this is demonstrated clearly when one of the young children discovers that his parents have lost their parental nurturing instincts, and have subjugated themselves to the radical and dangerous influences of a charismatic member of the extended family.\r\n\r\nAnyone who has had relationships with their parents damaged over politics, the 24-hour news cycle, talk radio and right or left wing influences may be able to relate to this. I know I certainly do. Worse, is the feeling that individual thoughts, reason, values have all been cast aside for the ideological and immoral agenda of a charismatic leader who intoxicates themselves by the power they wield, especially over others.\r\n\r\nAm I making this clearer?\r\n\r\nBut the funny thing about power is the inevitability of the pendulum's swing -- which should leave no doubt when one is reminded of what befell Romania's Ceaușescu, Libya's Gadaffi, Italy's Mussolini, Iraq's Hussein, and, obviously, Rome's Julius Caesar.\r\n\r\nAnd that might be the moral of **WEAPONS** -- that as intoxicating as power can be, there is a breaking point for all leaders who wield it where, ironically, the very legions of lemmings one controls may one day 'wake up' from their fugue states, bringing with them an irreversible whirlwind of destruction.\r\n\r\nNeedless to say, Creggers’ **WEAPONS** is a terrifying roller coaster ride that jolts and surprises with its rise and fall incidents as specific characters and events are explored. It's horrifying, harrowing, and sometimes funny (though the laughter I joined in on with the rest of the cinema crowd was that of the 'nervous' kind).\r\n\r\nI never felt comfortable during **WEAPONS** because of the thoroughly unpredictable direction the film was going and when Josh Brolin's character erupted with a "What the fuck!?" -- we all agreed!\r\n\r\n**WEAPONS** is bound to be many things for many people and I look forward to hearing other interpretations, but for now, this is undeniably Creggers’ best work. Garner, Brolin and Benedict Wong are standouts in this unforgettable film.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-30 01:42:41+03	2026-07-30 01:42:41+03
