6305	3328	18	4	It's a society where humanity has been largely subsumed into a digital world; where cyborgs are a norm and where people are now, essentially, dependant on their own technology for just about all aspects of their survival. In this virtual environment, the "Puppet Master" is the kingpin of the criminal world. It knows that by accessing the minds of those connected to the internet, it can obtain lots of juicy information to facilitate a spree of lucrative and threatening crime. Our very thoughts and dreams are now susceptible - and so the government creates "Section 9". Their job is to reverse-engineer some of these hacks and to try and trace the mastermind before it becomes unstoppable. What now ensues is not the finest example of animation you will ever see - indeed some of the artistry is disappointingly basic and linear. What makes this work is a sold story that questions the very soul of what it is to be an human being. It probes what makes us, well, us... Our identity, our personality - all are vulnerable to manipulation here by something adept at capitalising on the flaws and failings of each of us. Once these flood gates are open, is it even possible to arrest it as global interconnectivity makes control nigh on impossible. There are emerging elements of apocalypse to the plot - but they serve to illustrate the dangers of walking into convenience eyes open, but not looking - and there's some potent dialogue here about our complicity in our what could potentially be our own downfall. Can we necessarily spot truth from fiction any more? What is real and what is not? It's not your typical animé, but it does get you thinking and is very much worth watching.	\N	\N	pending	2024-02-04 14:27:59+03	2024-02-04 14:27:59+03
6306	4036	18	4	It's a bit of a linguistic faux-pas that gives their new android the name "Andrew" (Robin Williams) after it arrives at the home of "Sir" (Sam Neill), his wife (Wendy Crewson) and their two daughters. The younger of those is a little more engaged with this butler-cum-housekeeper but her sibling thinks it belongs on a scrapheap - sometimes with quite humorous results. As the family start to get used to their new gadget, though, they experience some surprising changes in attitudes and questions are raised about just what constitutes "humanity". He is sympathetic, creative, empathetic and those characteristics aren't wasted on the mother in whom he confides his desire for freedom. Isn't he happy? Why does he want to leave? Well "Sir" takes a bit of umbridge at this request but grants it - and so "Andrew" finds himself free to live his life as he sees fit whilst still remaining in contact with a family who are no less divided about him and his role than they were a generation (or two) ago. Meantime, he embarks on a quest to track down the other robots of his generation and that introduces him to scientist "Rupert" (Oliver Platt) who suggests that there are ways of changing his appearance to present an altogether more human appearance. This unnerves those of his human family but enables him to re-engage with the sceptical "Portia" (Embeth Davidtz) and so take his journey onto an whole new and emotional level. Williams always was good at crafting a character that could bring a tear from a glass eye, and aided by a solid Isaac Asimov story and a typically orchestral score from James Horner he manages to create a character in whom we could invest a little. Of course, some of it is just plain daft and as "Andrew" hits his bicentennial the societal environment in which he lives did seem totally unrealistic, but by that point it had stopped being a sci-fi story and become something more of a romance that I struggled a bit to believe in. It's essentially a story of evolution - human and technological, and it's also one that sets out clearly the potential for artificial intelligence to acquire the need for love and for the ability to give it too.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-08 14:16:07+03	2026-08-08 14:18:57+03
