6346	3503	18	4	Four young pranksters from Hell's Kitchen find themselves in a reform school after one of their scams - with an hotdog vendor's cart - ends in tragedy. No sooner have they arrived than the warder "Nokes" (Kevin Bacon) makes it known that these four lads are going to be providing him and his colleagues with some extra-curricular entertainment in the basement of the prison. The remainder of their sentence proves horrific and abusive and upon release they go their separate ways. Two - "Johnny" (Ron Eldard) and "Tommy" (Billy Crudup) continue with a life of petty crime whilst "Shakes" (Jason Patric) becomes a journalist and "Michael" (Brad Pitt) becomes an assistant DA. Then one afternoon in a bar the former pair stumble upon a man having his lunch - and it's noneother than their ertswhile persecutor. In this instance, spontaneity proves the mother of invention and swiftly the two men find themselves in court indicted for murder. It would seem to be a clear-cut case but the boys might yet have a few unexpected aces up their sleeves, especially when "Michael" ends up prosecuting the case and "Shakes" sets about proving that they boys were sexually assaulted on a regular basis by tracking down the other abusers ready to be examined by their own lawyer - the rather scruffily unimpressive "Snyder" (Dustin Hoffman). Meantime, the only man who ever really saw any good in these lads - "Fr. Bobby" (Robert De Niro) might also be able to help out too - though in a way that might compromise some of his vows and spiritual integrity. Against some fairly overwhelming evidence, what scope in the justice system is there for a defence based along the lines of an eye for an eye? Though the headliners fare strongly here here, I felt it really the younger versions of themselves that stole the acting plaudits - especially Brad Renfro's younger version of "Michael" and Jonathan Tucker's "Tommy" and with Bacon on really quite menacing form as the odious guard we don't need to see what we know to have gone on to these lads. Hoffman also delivers quirkily like some sort of hybrid character crafted by Billy Wilder meets Alexandre Dumas and the whole courtroom drama elements put the principles of justice as opposed to the law under quite a spotlight. The pacing of this drama is a little bit juddery towards the conclusion as it struggles a little to avoid "Perry Mason" territory, but the later scene between De Niro and Pitt is the kind of cat and mouse stuff that you rarely see, and it tops of quite a tough but well executed film to watch.	\N	\N	pending	2026-09-15 20:56:37+03	2026-09-15 20:56:37+03
6347	12717	48	4	FULL SPOILER-FREE REVIEW @ https://www.msbreviews.com/movie-reviews/last-night-in-soho-spoiler-free-review\r\n\r\n"Last Night in Soho is incredibly captivating throughout the entire runtime, but Edgar Wright's all-in in the insane third act is definitely going to generate a divisive response.\r\n\r\nStory-wise, the compelling protagonists - brilliantly interpreted by Thomasin McKenzie and Anya Taylor-Joy - follow intriguing arcs holding unexpected revelations that ultimately make sense with the overall narrative. Stunning cinematography and production design come together to create a visually jaw-dropping 1960s London, which becomes an even more entertaining place to visit due to Steve Price's addictive music.\r\n\r\nHowever, the last act feels a sudden hurry to tie every knot through overwhelming editing, hasty pacing, and careless transitions, which work against what the film had been until that point.\r\n\r\nI commend Wright's commitment and extreme dedication to his vision, but the restraint shown previously should have also been part of the conclusion."\r\n\r\nRating: B+	\N	\N	pending	2021-11-01 00:48:18+03	2021-11-01 00:48:18+03
