6727	13173	1077	4	This is based on Fredrik Backman's absolutely brilliant and touching novel A Man Called Ove, and is also a remake of the 2015 Swedish film that earned two Oscar nominations. Of course, the movie is just a shadow of the novel; no adaptation could capture everything Backman achieves on the page. But the writers and director Marc Forster did a very decent job of paring the story down to essential plot and successfully transplanting it to America, finding the universality in Backman's grumpy Swedish widower.\r\n\r\nWhat carries the film is Tom Hanks and Mariana Treviño. Their performances and chemistry are genuinely affecting; Treviño brings warmth and persistent cheerfulness that slowly chips away at Otto's armor, and the two actors create a believable relationship that shifts from antagonism to unlikely friendship. Hanks commits fully to the role, finding moments of real pathos beneath the curmudgeonly exterior.\r\n\r\nThat said, I honestly think Harrison Ford or Clint Eastwood would have made a better Ove/Otto. Hanks, for all his skill, couldn't quite convey the full complexity of the character in Backman's novel. There's a certain hardness, a lifetime of disappointment calcified into bitter principle, that requires an actor comfortable inhabiting genuine unlikability. Hanks is too inherently sympathetic; even when playing difficult, he signals the good heart underneath. Ove needs to feel truly impossible before we understand why.\r\n\r\nStill, this is a competent, heartfelt adaptation that captures enough of Backman's spirit to be worthwhile, even if it can't match either the novel or, likely, the Swedish original.\r\n\r\n7/10: a decent remake that succeeds on the strength of its leads, even if it doesn't fully capture the novel's depth.	\N	\N	pending	2026-06-24 19:49:11+03	2026-06-24 19:49:11+03
6728	1839	33	4	The Prince and the Pauper with Eddie & Dan on sparkling form.\r\n\r\nMortimer & Randolph Duke are two repugnantly rich brothers, they make a bet that sees the role reversal of top toff yuppie, Louis Winthorpe, and wise cracking street hustler bum, Billy Ray Valentine. That's about the strength of the films plot, yet it makes for a very funny film that crackles with glee due to it's excellently written script. Watching the respective characters rise and fall respectively creates laughs aplenty whilst asking the question of how we all would cope in similar circumstances?\r\n\r\nSure the film does beat one over the head with its social message, we are in no doubt from the off about the gap between the rich and the poor, and yes the colour of a persons skin also rears its ugly head here to make this one of the more braver comedies of the 80s. Billy Ray Valentine (a brilliant dual performance from Eddie Murphy) is elevated up the social ladder, he becomes a force in industry, but as the progression enthrals him it also makes him aware that the things at the top end of the ladder aren't exactly stand up doings. Winthorpe (a perfectly casted Dan Ackroyd) drops further down the social ladder and resorts to behaviour that nobody from the upper echelons could ever have dreamed he would be capable of - it's only an encounter with prostitute Ophelia (Jamie Lee Curtis at the peak of her sexiness), and her good heart, that starts to see an upturn in his now dead bottom fortunes. \r\n\r\nThe gags come thick and fast, both verbally (Murphy on fire) and visually, as the film sees the whole cast fusing together to create a cracking comedy. Come the denouement on Wall Street we are privy to a joyous and savage turn of events that ice the clever Christmas cake we have just digested. It does have an 80s sheen about it, and viewing now in post 9/11 times it's got a tint of nostalgia value to it, but really it's all about the script, the stars and a kick in the eye for those who think nothing of treading on the people below them, enjoy. 8/10	\N	\N	pending	2018-12-23 05:30:05+03	2024-05-16 18:22:20+03
