6769	14369	4356	4	If you crossed the Brothers Grimm's classic 1819 short story ***The Bremen Town Musicians*** with a Klingon Warrior's rite of passage, and set that within the Predator universe -- you end up with writer and director Dan Trachtenberg's **PREDATOR: BADLANDS**.\r\n\r\nOne cannot argue that Trachtenberg, who is single-handedly ushering in a Predator renaissance beginning with his 2022 **PREY**, followed by this year's animated, gory actioner **PREDATOR: KILLER OF KILLERS** has [(which I reviewed here)](https://letterboxd.com/fixateandbinge/film/predator-killer-of-killers/) pulled off a brilliant Predator hat-trick for fans of the franchise.\r\n\r\nIn only three years Trachtenberg has brushed away over a decade of accumulated dust on a franchise that seemed destined for the dustbin of ignominious cinema oblivion (hastened, in large part, by those incongruous Alien matchups that tried, and failed, to follow the Universal Studios formula of Dracula versus Frankenstein, Wolf Man, etc) -- and has reintroduced the franchise to a new generation of fans.\r\n\r\nIn **BADLANDS**, Trachentberg plunges viewers firmly into the warrior culture of a clan of Predators (which we learn are called Yautja) steeped in 'only the strong deserve to live" ethos. Caught up in this warrior code is Dek (Dimitrius Schuster-Koloamatangi) -- who in his bid to earn his place as a bona fide Yautja for his clan, escapes to a 'Killer Planet' to subdue an unkillable creature, and avenge a heartbreaking loss in the bargain. Two birds, one stone.\r\n\r\nOutclassed and struggling to survive on the hostile planet -- Dek accepts help from one of the most unlikeliest of sources -- a severely damaged synthetic android Thia (Elle Fanning) --who has an interesting connection to the world of **ALIEN** (which I feel is unnecessary to maintain at this point).\r\n\r\nTogether they navigate the perils of the treacherous world they are on, with Dek doing something we haven't seen before from a Predator: set aside his Yautja machismo in favor of working with others. In the process of doing this, a mutually beneficial friendship develops between Dek, Thia and an indigenous creature that will tug at your heartstrings, adding some comic relief to what would otherwise feel like a brutal, one-note slog.\r\n\r\nFor anyone despairing over the rating -- PG-13 -- given the barbarism I watched on screen, this is every bit as gory and graphic as any of the previous **PREDATOR** films. I understand the rating -- I do, but for anyone laboring under the assumption that Trachtenberg 'nerfed' the film's violence, I'm happy to say that is not the case.\r\n\r\nAs a sci-fi, actioner -- this is a good addition in the **PREDATOR** franchise, but I remain doubtful of the efficacy of keeping the door open to incorporating anything from the **ALIEN** franchise. Trachtenberg's **PREDATOR** franchise is working just fine on its own.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-30 01:25:33+03	2026-07-30 01:25:33+03
