6872	4944	18	4	This has shades of "Horatio Hornblower" to it, but is much grittier. Russell Crowe delivers a strong, convincing, portrayal of the doughty captain "Aubrey", in command of HMS "Surprise" and charged with tracking down a French privateer that is raiding the vital British whaling fleet during the Napoleonic wars. Coming up against an equally courageous and skilful French captain and engaging in a lethal game of cat and mouse, he must outmanoeuvre this cunning foe before he and his crew end up in Davy Jones' Locker. The film nods to the social issues and superstitions of living on a 19th Century frigate and to bravery (and cowardice) in a subtle yet plausible way. Above all, it's an adventure film and there is plenty of action as we go. A strong ensemble cast in support (though Paul Bettany as the doctor and "Aubrey's" best friend on this voyage didn't quite wash with me) complement some great maritime cinematography; a good score and Peter Weir's able and exciting direction.	\N	\N	pending	2023-09-03 20:45:09+03	2023-09-03 20:45:09+03
6873	4944	1077	4	This is superbly crafted entertainment, the kind of filmmaking that reminds you what cinema can do when every element is working in concert. Peter Weir has gone to extreme lengths to create a cinematic world that absorbs the viewer completely, achieving that rare suspension of disbelief where you forget you're watching a movie and simply inhabit the HMS Surprise alongside its crew. And he was quite successful here. It's more than impressive; it's immersive.\r\n\r\nThe authenticity of the costumes and sets, the daily issues and rhythms of sailors' lives, all felt perfect. I can't personally vouch for the period accuracy, but from what little I know, it seemed quite accomplished. What matters is that Weir made you believe it, made the creaking wood and salt spray and hierarchies of shipboard life feel absolutely real. The sound design deserves particular praise. Thanks to Christopher Gordon, Iva Davies, and Richard Tognetti, the soundtrack is perfect for this film, neither overwhelming nor absent, just present in the way good music should be.\r\n\r\nRussell Crowe and Paul Bettany were amazing. Their friendship felt very real and supportive, the kind of bond between two intelligent men who respect each other's differences. Crowe's Captain Aubrey is all duty and command, while Bettany's Dr. Maturin is curiosity and science, and somehow they balance each other without conflict feeling forced. As far as I could tell, the entire supporting cast did a fantastic job of staying in character, creating a lived-in world of sailors who felt like individuals rather than extras.\r\n\r\nWhat matters here is the craft, the art of creating a world and inviting us into it with such confidence and skill that we never question whether we belong there. Master and Commander is a reminder that pure entertainment, executed at the highest level, is its own form of cinematic achievement.	\N	\N	pending	2026-03-18 21:43:50+03	2026-03-18 21:43:50+03
