8655	4217	18	3	Perhaps if Wolfgang Petersen had just done without all the dysfunctional family stuff and just taken this crew on their ill-fated mission right from the start, I might have enjoyed this more. As it is, we spend almost an hour learning all about the relationships of "Billy" (George Clooney), "Bobby" (Mark Wahlberg) and of the rivalry between "Murph" (John C. Reilly) and "Sully" (William Fichter) and to be honest, I wasn't in the mood for all that relationship melodrama. However, when we do finally put to sea in their tiny little ship we see a little of just how subsistance this industry can be. If the catches are good, then the cash flows from Michael Ironside's frugal and grumpy owner "Brown". If the catch isn't good, then their share of nothing is only a percentage of nothing. "Billy" hasn't really been pulling in the big hauls of late and so is under pressure to deliver for his boss and his crew - so he heads to an area at the very edge of their endurance where he hopes there will be fish. What he doesn't know, but that we do, is that the excited meteorologists are upgrading a tropical storm to something altogether more perilous for the folks on this boat. With the poor old coastguard desperately trying to rescue other idiots who don't follow the forecasters advice and the storms all gathering for their Christmas party, it looks like "Billy" et al are in for quite a struggle if they are to make it back to the safety of an harbour where their previous problems don't seem quite so urgent. This film is all about the visual effects and they are fairly spectacular as the clouds gather and that wave that toppled the "Poseidon" (1972) makes it's long awaited cinema reappearance. Indeed, the last twenty minutes are a testament to some astonishing visual creativity augmented by some solid audio editing and a genuine sense of menace that doesn't suggest any sort of rescue is on the horizon - not that they can see any horizon. The acting and writing matter not here, it's just another one of those movies that reminds us that should nature decide to be shot of humanity, it has plenty in it's arsenal.	\N	\N	pending	2026-06-20 18:09:53+03	2026-06-20 18:14:10+03
