8665	3347	1315	2	Adapting video games to movies has always been a rough ride, but the 90s gave us a relic that shaped a whole generation. However, taking off the nostalgia goggles exposes a lot of flaws. Revisiting this first attempt to bring arcade fighting to the big screen shows that simply staying true to the game's premise isn't enough to make a solid film. Once you strip away the childhood affection, you're left with technical and narrative holes that time hasn't forgiven.\r\n\r\nThe movie suffers from a major identity crisis from start to finish. Director Paul W.S. Anderson can't seem to pick a lane, bouncing awkwardly between overly serious melodrama and unintentional slapstick. Without nostalgia to protect it, it feels like an uncohesive, poorly written mess that just throws superficial references at the screen to get a cheap pop from the audience. It survived back then mostly on its marketing, but it completely fails as a legitimate movie for adults today.\r\n\r\nIt also heavily disrespects the original lore. Fan-favorite rivals like Scorpion and Sub-Zero are reduced to voiceless henchmen, completely ignoring their fascinating backstory. Other legendary fighters are treated as disposable punching bags just to make the heroes look good, turning the fights into lazy, empty spectacles. Sonya Blade gets it the worst. She starts off as a badass, independent Special Forces agent but is slowly downgraded into a tired damsel in distress who has to be rescued by the guys. Her fight scenes lack the energy of Liu Kang's, totally wasting the franchise's toughest female character.\r\n\r\nAnderson also drops the ball on the action. He struggles to build tension or maintain a good pace, resulting in choppy editing that cuts away right when a scene needs to breathe. His lack of experience with martial arts really shows—the camera angles are usually wrong, and the confusing edits try to hide the actors' slow, stiff choreography. The mud fight between Sonya and Kano is a perfect example of this, looking more like a sloppy bar brawl than a mystical tournament between deadly warriors.\r\n\r\nThe acting doesn't help either, often bordering on amateurish. Linden Ashby's Johnny Cage lacks the arrogant charm the role needs, while Bridgette Wilson-Sampras and Talisa Soto deliver their lines like robots. Robin Shou brings the right physical skills as Liu Kang, but he lacks the emotional weight of a leading man. Even Cary-Hiroyuki Tagawa's Shang Tsung is sabotaged by a script full of shallow catchphrases. But the biggest miscast is Christopher Lambert as Raiden. He plays the Thunder God as a raspy, apathetic guy who just smiles ironically, totally ruining the gravity of the mentor role and weakening the movie's main conflict.\r\n\r\nOne of the most unforgivable flaws is the total lack of gore. The studio completely sanitized the game's famous, blood-soaked Fatalities to get a PG-13 rating and sell more tickets to kids. Because of this, the finishing moves are terribly anticlimactic. Without those lethal stakes, the inherently dark universe turns into a generic, low-budget martial arts flick, losing the subversive edge that made the arcade games so popular in the first place.\r\n\r\nOn a technical level, it’s pretty disastrous. The constant 90s techno soundtrack gets exhausting and ruins any chance of building real tension, making the whole thing feel like a long, dated music video. The low budget is painfully obvious in the sets, which look more like abandoned warehouses with cheap fog than an epic alien realm. The CGI for Reptile looks like a primitive console game, and the costumes look like fresh, clean Halloween outfits rather than real combat gear. Even the practical Goro animatronic, while ambitious for the time, ends up looking stiff, badly dubbed, and unintentionally funny.\r\n\r\nUltimately, the movie collapses under its own limitations. What should have been a heavy martial arts epic gets ruined by lifeless acting, dated effects, and bad action choreography. The studio's censorship gutted the franchise's aggressive edge. Still, watching a movie is a subjective experience. While a critical analysis exposes its broken structure, you might still have fun watching it. It’s a great case study on how hard it is to adapt a game into a movie, so watch it yourself—your nostalgia might forgive its flaws a lot easier than I did.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-13 05:56:21+03	2026-08-13 05:56:21+03
8666	3720	101	4	**Serious harmonization problems.**\r\n\r\nAfter the enormous success of Mr. Bean, it was reasonably predictable that, sooner or later, we would see Rowan Atkinson performing his usual antics in a film production. However, despite the actor's efforts, the truth is that it would never be the same, nor could it be, and sometimes the jokes are so forced that they simply lose their effectiveness.\r\n\r\nIn this film, Bean found work at the National Gallery in London. A job that is apparently safe, if we consider that the character gets into trouble even with the simplest tasks: he is a watchman and sits in a chair while visitors and tourists walk around. The problem is that, in the case of Bean, we know that things are going to get complicated almost by magic: and the gallery directors are very happy when the curators of a Californian museum acquire a remarkable painting by an American author and an expert to talk about her at a presentation ceremony: Bean is the one the directors most want to see behind her back, so they don't even hesitate. From here, disaster is waiting to happen.\r\n\r\nLet's be honest: the film is funny and works reasonably well. It's a good quality comedy, and it was also successful at the box office (although that's not synonymous with quality, because it's also true that there are many much worse films, like “Ted”, which also sold well). We can't point out defects that it doesn't have at all. For me, the biggest defect of this film is the audience, which created very high expectations at the expense of the gigantic success of the fourteen episode miniseries that Atkinson created in the 90s. Anyone waiting to have the same experience with This film will always be disappointing. Things aren't the same, they don't work the same way and everything is a little more exaggerated and forced. However, it would be difficult for an American production to make a feature-length film with Bean any other way.\r\n\r\nThe film takes great care and attention with the sets, cinematography, costumes and effects used, but it is still within the “standard” of light films that the USA released at this time. It's nothing truly exceptional. Throughout the film, situations occur that attempt to recreate Atkinson's style of humor, as he does his best to avoid talking, but still has to do so occasionally. We can say that the actor made a huge effort, but that he also encountered problems adapting his recipe to the North American cinematic style. It's like trying to dress a child in an adult man's costume: it will look bizarre, disharmonious and ugly, but he's dressed.\r\n\r\nIn between, we also have to positively highlight the work of Peter MacNicoll, and of course, being a historian and an art lover, I have to congratulate the use of the occasion to make known to the general public a magnificent painting that really exists: Arrangement in Gray and Black nº 1. It was painted by James Whistler and can be seen at the Orsay Museum in Paris.	\N	\N	pending	2024-03-05 22:01:26+03	2024-03-05 22:01:26+03
