9088	14664	1315	3	Going back to a classic is always a slippery slope. When I sat down to watch the highly anticipated sequel to *The Devil Wears Prada*, I was worried it would just be a pale copy of the original. Unfortunately, the new movie tries so hard to balance the comfort of nostalgia with the decline of print media that it ends up with a bit of an identity crisis. The direction feels strangely bureaucratic, as if the person behind the camera was just checking boxes for an engagement algorithm instead of bringing a unique vision. This is incredibly obvious in the visuals, too. The gritty, frantic warmth of early 2000s New York is gone, replaced by a sterile, overly polished digital look. The luxurious sets that used to feel seductive and oppressive now just feel cold, making the film look more like a generic streaming movie than a pop culture trendsetter.\r\n\r\nThe script basically falls into the "soft reboot" trap. It recycles the exact same narrative beats and conflicts from the first movie, completely ignoring that these characters are in entirely different stages of their lives. Dealing with the brutal transition to the digital age and the collapse of the print market was the perfect natural path to take, but the execution is incredibly shallow. AI and social media are handled in a way that feels almost cartoonish, like an out-of-touch older executive trying to explain how the internet works. To make matters worse, the pacing really drags in the second act. The story gets bogged down by boring contract negotiations and forced romantic subplots that have zero chemistry and kill any sense of urgency. Everything is wrapped up way too easily in the end, making the whole thing feel like a very safe, predictable rough draft.\r\n\r\nBringing Meryl Streep, Anne Hathaway, and Emily Blunt back together seemed like a surefire hit, but the reality on screen is pretty disappointing. The undeniable spark from the original is just gone, and their interactions feel so cold and rehearsed that it almost seems like they filmed their scenes in separate rooms. The script's biggest mistake was trying to awkwardly humanize Miranda Priestly. The ruthless, terrifying editor we loved to hate has been watered down into a tired, vulnerable executive, which completely strips her commands of their weight and ruins the tension. Emily Blunt’s immense talent is also totally wasted. While Andy has clearly matured, Emily is stuck in time, acting as a recycled punching bag who just repeats her bitter complaints from two decades ago without any real character growth. Throw in a new generation of assistants who lack any real charm, and it's clear the brilliant chemistry of the original was left in the past.\r\n\r\nUltimately, the movie struggles to find its tone, awkwardly bouncing between 2000s dark comedy and a melancholic drama about the publishing world. It’s a victim of its own legacy, playing it too safe and prioritizing commercial demand over any genuine creative vision. But hey, my take shouldn't be the final word. If you're just looking for a cozy hit of nostalgia and aren't too worried about a flawless plot, seeing these familiar faces again might hit you completely differently. I definitely recommend watching it and drawing your own conclusions—your personal connection to the classic might help you look past these flaws and enjoy the return to Runway in a way my critical eye just couldn't.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-31 04:40:35+03	2026-07-31 04:40:35+03
