9579	14663	1315	3	Watching modern cinema try to resurrect 80s franchises has become an exercise in patience and, usually, pure frustration. When a new *Masters of the Universe* movie was announced, the promise was to bring back the grandeur of that world using today's technology. But instead of honoring the original, the studio delivered an exhausting, soulless adventure that feels ashamed of its own roots. Rather than embracing the colorful, campy fantasy of Eternia, the film constantly hides behind worn-out, self-deprecating irony. It’s terrified to take its source material seriously. This heavy reliance on cheap nostalgia and easter eggs might try to pander to older fans, but it leaves newcomers completely lost, resulting in a movie with a massive identity crisis.\r\n\r\nDirector Travis Knight usually brings a lot of heart to his projects, but he really struggles with an epic of this scale. The direction feels messy, and the shaky camerawork during action scenes makes it almost impossible to tell who is fighting who. Add in a heavy reliance on unfinished green screens, and the movie completely loses any sense of physical weight. You don't feel the impact of a sword strike; you just see a blur of digital lights. The script, written by Chris Butler, Aaron Nee, and Adam Nee, only drags things down further. It's a bloated take on the hero's journey that constantly ruins moments of genuine drama with forced, cynical jokes. To make matters worse, the characters don't actually talk to each other—they just deliver robotic, cringey motivational speeches that kill any chance of an emotional connection.\r\n\r\nOriginally, this franchise was all about fun, colorful escapism: a giant barbarian fighting a talking skeleton. But the writers insist on forcing dense social commentary and philosophical dilemmas into a plastic universe that just can't handle them. This completely tanks the pacing, especially in the second act, which is bogged down by pointless political subplots that do nothing for the main story. Honestly, the editors could have easily chopped off forty minutes without losing anything important, and we would have had a much tighter, far less tedious movie.\r\n\r\nThe cast doesn't help either, as they all seem to be running on autopilot with zero chemistry. Nicholas Galitzine tries his best to carry the lead, but Prince Adam is written as so passive and boring that he loses all his classic nobility. We spend way too much time with his angsty human side before He-Man finally shows up, and Galitzine looks visibly uncomfortable delivering his artificial lines. Camila Mendes is also totally wasted. Her version of Teela is stripped of her original bravery and reduced to a bitter character with terrible dialogue, only to be completely sidelined as a spectator in the final act. And seeing an actor of Idris Elba's caliber reduced to a glorified extra as Duncan is tough to watch. He’s just there to dump exposition and drop tired one-liners instead of acting as a meaningful mentor.\r\n\r\nThen there's Jared Leto’s Skeletor, which is an absolute disaster. He turns the franchise’s most iconic villain into a hysterical, theatrical joke with a bizarre voice that totally takes you out of the movie. Because he's so over-the-top and never feels like a credible threat, all the tension in the film completely falls apart.\r\n\r\nOn a technical level, the movie is just as exhausting. The aggressive, guitar-heavy soundtrack never gives you a moment to breathe, often drowning out important dialogue. Visually, instead of a cool mix of sci-fi and medieval fantasy, Eternia looks cheap, sterile, and generic. The costumes look like amateur cosplay, lacking any real grit or wear and tear from a world at war. Everything is so polished and smothered in fake-looking CGI that the big epic battles end up feeling like an outdated video game cutscene, with actors clearly just floating in a green screen room.\r\n\r\nIn the end, *Masters of the Universe* completely fumbles its chance to be a massive summer blockbuster. Throwing a ton of money at a movie can't buy it a soul. It's frustrating to see a mythology with so much potential reduced to poorly timed jokes and bad CGI because the filmmakers didn't have the guts to just embrace the fantasy. But hey, movies are subjective. What I see as a bloated disaster might be a fun, nostalgic weekend ride for you. If you're curious, grab a ticket and see for yourself—sometimes ripping apart a giant mess of a movie is the best part of the whole experience.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-31 04:44:30+03	2026-07-31 04:44:30+03
9580	14663	1568	4	At the beginning of the movie I was really not happy that it was that silly from the get-go. I personally would've really wished to get a "real" in-Eternia movie and not a "real world" thing.\r\n\r\nBut relatively quickly, the movie did start to work for me. I really enjoyed how fitting the outfits of the named characters were done, especially Trapjaw, Man-at-Arms, Beastman and Mekaneck.\r\n\r\nWhile the "real world" humor was okay for me but not something I would've needed, the humor about some ridiculous parts of the franchise, like how the characters got their names, poses imitating the TV show poses and such, were really done well.\r\n\r\nDespite the differences to the cartoon show, it really feels like the people who did the movie actually enjoyed working with the franchise.\r\n\r\nThe story is fine, a typical "origin story" with some plot-armor and deus-ex-machina events, but contrary to some other movies, these actually fit the "stereotypical warriors of light" theme that He-Man ultimately is.\r\n\r\nSo, if you can accept that the beginning of the movie has the "real world" shenanigans and humor, I think you'll enjoy it as a fan of the old TV show.\\\r\nAnd it could certainly work as a "prequel" on how He-Man became the "more serious warrior of light" later on.\r\n\r\nNeeds more Cringer, though!\r\n\r\nBut yeah, overall, an entertaining movie if you don't take your Masters of the Universe too seriously, many really fun eastereggs, and I hope that, despite the "meh" box office results, we will get a sequel or spin-off at some point.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-05 10:01:23+03	2026-08-05 10:01:23+03
9581	14663	48	3	Check out my full review @ https://www.manuelsbento.com/masters-of-the-universe-2026-nicholas-galitzine-powers-a-gleefully-silly-slightly-overstuffed-return-to-eternia/\r\n\r\nRating: B-\r\n\r\nMASTERS OF THE UNIVERSE surprised me: unapologetically silly, self-aware, and anchored by a cast that's clearly having fun — Nicholas Galitzine's earnest He-Man, Camila Mendes' scene-stealing Teela, and a gloriously committed Jared Leto as Skeletor. Travis Knight balances camp and epic fantasy better than expected, and Eternia genuinely feels like a real place. The catch? It's simply too long, and the comedy sometimes undercuts moments that deserved to breathe. Still, a solidly entertaining time at the movies.	\N	\N	pending	2026-09-06 17:44:13+03	2026-09-06 17:44:13+03
9582	2134	33	4	He went walkabout.  \r\n\r\nNew York reporter Sue Charlton hears of a guy in the outback of Australia who survived an attack by a crocodile. For research she meets up with "Crocodile Mick Dundee" and spends time with him out in dangerous Bush Country. Finding a rapport during their time together, Sue convinces Mick to go back with her to New York, which brings interesting results as Mick becomes a big hit by treating the Big Apple, and all that comes his way, the same as he would the Outback.\r\n\r\nCrocodile Dundee has a standard fish out of water comedy premise, yet with a number of truly funny sequences and an appealing turn from Paul Hogan as Dundee, it became a monster smash hit that the cinema watching public lapped up with glee. In a decade that is often considered or debated to be the worst for film, it may just be that cinema goers were desperate for a pick me up movie? Possibly, but undoubtedly Crocodile Dundee most assuredly is that type of escapist piece. However, to give that credence would, I feel, be doing it a disservice, for in spite of the rickety concept and the obviousness of where we will ultimately end up, it has bundles of earthy charm, a charm that many can identify with.\r\n\r\nAs Dundee goes about his way, meeting pimps, transsexuals and muggers et al, they are not only very funny scenes, they are also points of reference to the ever changing way of the Continents. Not that the film doesn't come dangerously close to falling into a sugary rom-com mire, but with a strong performance from Linda Kozlowski as Sue, and Hogan introducing an icon to 80s cinema, Crocodile Dundee safely hits the target that it was surely aiming for. Besides, the love story here is very easy to get on side with, to support it and hanker for this opposites attract coupling to work out.\r\n\r\nTwo sequels would follow, the first one was a retread reversal and just about passable, the second one, after a gap of 13 years, was bad and evidence that the joke had long since passed. Crocodile Dundee 86 holds up well as the escapist piece of cinema that it is. A nice film to revisit every other year, for it be simple, warm, and yes, I'll say it again, damn funny. 8/10	\N	\N	pending	2019-01-20 01:45:36+03	2024-05-16 18:22:20+03
