9907	20	6793	3	**A Great Premise That Never Quite Lives Up to Its Potential**\r\n\r\nThe End of Oak Street has one of those premises that immediately sells you on the movie: an ordinary suburban neighborhood suddenly finds itself surrounded by prehistoric wilderness and dinosaurs. Unfortunately, the movie itself never becomes quite as interesting as that setup.\r\n\r\nThere’s definitely fun to be had here. The dinosaur sequences are entertaining, Anne Hathaway and Ewan McGregor are good, and the movie moves along quickly enough that it never really becomes boring. I also appreciated that it feels more like an old-fashioned adventure movie than another attempt to build a giant franchise.\r\n\r\nThe problem is that it spends a surprising amount of time on fairly generic family drama when there’s a much more interesting story happening outside the house. The characters never become compelling enough to justify how much attention is given to their problems, and some of the emotional moments feel forced rather than earned.\r\n\r\nIt also wears its Spielberg influences very heavily on its sleeve. There’s nothing wrong with trying to capture that 1980s Amblin feeling, but at times it feels less inspired by those movies than like it’s deliberately trying to recreate them.\r\n\r\nStill, dinosaurs attacking suburbia is a pretty difficult concept to make completely boring, and there are enough good sequences here to make it worth watching. I just wish the movie had embraced the weirdness of its premise a little more.\r\n\r\n**6/10**	\N	\N	pending	2026-09-04 22:58:25+03	2026-09-05 11:06:59+03
