10064	8845	18	4	I thought there something of the "Rapunzel" to this story. "Anna" is a frail twelve year old girl who has never quite fitted in, and who has been sent by her caring foster-mother from her home in the city to spend the summer in a quiet town by the sea in the hope that it may have some recuperative effect. Whilst exploring one day, she encounters an home aptly named "Marsh House" inhabited by the mysterious and solitary "Marnie". Both are shy and reluctant to come out of their shells, but are equally intrigued by the other and so slowly a bond begins to establish between the pair. We know already something of "Anna" and her complicated past, but "Marnie" is much more enigmatic and as the story unfolds there becomes something more of the fairy tale to this blonde-locked girl looking from the window of her blue room. As her holiday continues, "Anna" determines to find out more about her new friend and in the process a discover a little more about herself and her own past. This probably isn't the most noted of the Ghibli films and for the first half, this rather delicately drawn animation rather ponderously meanders along, but once we pass the half way mark the story starts to gain momentum and the characters and the mystery deepen to present us with a more interesting hybrid of friendship and fantasy. The narrative delicately treads on the eggshells of both girls' loneliness and imagination without ever really committing to "Marnie" being a real girl or an imaginary friend in the vein of "Harvey" the rabbit. To add to the slightly muted but effective colours, there is a generous and often luxuriant score to accompany the quirkiness of the tale and despite the presence of plenty of clues as to just what is going to happen, there remains enough of a puzzle to keep us on tenterhooks until a conclusion that wasn't quite what I was anticipating. It's an effortless film to let wash over you and is well worth a look.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-04 10:48:53+03	2026-0