10704	14653	1315	4	Mixing cute farm animals with a cold-blooded murder sounds like a recipe for disaster, and honestly, this movie suffers from a massive identity crisis. The idea of a flock of sheep trying to solve their owner's death is wildly absurd, but on screen, it ends up being a pretty fractured experience. The animation team did an amazing job making the animals look physically real—their wool and movements blend perfectly into the beautiful, real-world English countryside. However, once these hyper-realistic sheep start moving their human-like lips to speak, the film dives straight into the uncanny valley. It’s super distracting and turns what should be a visual win into something a bit creepy, making it hard to actually connect with the mystery.\r\n\r\nDespite the weird visuals, the voice cast absolutely nails it and brings a lot of heart to the story. Julia Louis-Dreyfus is fantastic as Lily, the sharp and empathetic leader of the flock, while Bryan Cranston brings a grounded, lonely weight to his character. Veterans like Patrick Stewart and Regina Hall give the sheep so much personality, creating some genuinely funny moments as the animals try to make sense of human behavior. Unfortunately, the actual human cast is completely wasted. It’s baffling to see huge stars like Hugh Jackman (whose screen time is basically a cameo) and Emma Thompson reduced to flat, exposition-dumping caricatures. Having such an A-list live-action cast just to use them as simple roadblocks for the sheep feels like a huge missed opportunity.\r\n\r\nThe biggest structural issue here is that the movie just can’t figure out what it wants to be. It constantly struggles to balance the innocent perspective of the sheep with the heavy reality of grief and murder, leading to some really jarring emotional whiplash. While the basic framework of a classic whodunit is there, anyone who's seen a few thrillers will figure out the killer way too early. To make matters worse, the middle of the film really drags. It spends way too much time on repetitive jokes about the sheep trying to communicate with the locals instead of actually advancing the plot, turning the second act into a bit of a patience test before things finally get moving again.\r\n\r\nWhen it's finally time to wrap things up, the script ditches its quirky, clever vibe and goes full-on melodramatic. The director tries to force tears with heavy speeches and a swelling soundtrack, which feels manipulative and totally unearned compared to the smart humor of the first half. Adapting Leonie Swann's weird and wonderful book was never going to be easy, and the movie definitely hits some very noticeable highs and lows. Still, the premise is so original that it might just justify the watch. It’s one of those bizarre cinematic experiments you just have to watch with an open mind to decide for yourself if this unconventional mystery actually works for you.	\N	\N	pending	2026-09-18 04:43:47+03	2026-09-18 04:43:47+03
