10946	14665	4723	4	The film strikes a great balance, delivering clever narrative tie-backs to Evil Dead Rise alongside a subtle, underlying connection to the lore of the original films. Rather than resting on nostalgia, it uses those classic elements to build a story that feels fresh while remaining distinctly true to the franchise's roots. \r\n\r\nThe shift in setting works brilliantly here. Moving the terror out of Rise’s cramped city apartment block and into a sprawling, secluded farmhouse gives the film a completely different kind of claustrophobia. The rural atmosphere and isolated location gave off massive Resident Evil 7: Biohazard Baker family vibes. That uncomfortable sense of being trapped with a deeply unhinged, hostile family dynamic long before the supernatural madness even takes over. \r\n\r\nWhen the Deadites do unleash, the film delivers in spades. Modern Evil Dead entries have really amped up the brutal, relentless nature of the gore, and Burn is no exception. What makes it work so well is the sheer tactile impact of the set pieces. Between the aggressive sound design and a reliance on heavy practical effects over cheap digital tricks, every injury, splash of blood, and horrific transformation feels uncomfortably real and punishing. \r\n\r\nIt’s really encouraging to see movies like this succeeding, especially since horror felt like it was going through a bit of a dry spell recently. We’re finally getting a steady stream of inventive, high-quality genre films that actually respect the audience and make the trip to the cinema worthwhile. \r\n\r\nI’d highly recommend Evil Dead Burn to any horror fan. The best way to experience these modern entries is to disconnect them from Raimi’s 80s originals. Those were iconic products of their time, whereas this current era is leaning into dark, visceral, unfiltered horror. Both eras are brilliant in their own right, and Burn proves this franchise still has plenty of gas in the tank.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-07 23:39:23+03	2026-08-08 03:55:01+03
