11003	13293	18	4	This isn't so much reading, (w)riting and 'rithmetic as rise, roar and revolt as is depicts a story of one man's uprising against the brutality of the Raj. The seeds of resentment have been bubbling under for many a year by the time the governor and his wife stop off at a small village. There, the young "Malli" (Twinkle Sharma) puts some designs on the hand of "Mrs. Scott" (Alison Doody) who is so impressed that she tells her husband (Ray Stevenson) that she wants the girl as a plaything. Despite the protestations of the girl's petrified mother, her wish is his command and off she is duly taken - with fatal results. Meantime, with revolution brewing elsewhere the British are relying on their fearsome policeman "Rama Raju" (Ajay Devgn) - a one-man force whom they appreciate when it suits them but who is never given his due recognition. That chance might come if he can track down "Bheem" (NTR) - a tribal leader who is proving to be more of a thorn in the side of the administration - and bring him to justice in any way he can. It's a train incident that sees the two men meet for the first time as they both battle to save a young girl from the fire and the waters of the lake, and from that incident a friendship emerges - but that's before they know just who the other is. What chance their friendship might survive otherwise? Right from the beginning, this historical epic manages to mix the mythology of the Gond with the cultural, economic and political issues facing a 1920s India under the control of a megalomaniac Imperialist. The visual effects are every bit as stylishly presented as anything we'd see from Hollywood or Pinewood and the pace of the film rarely stops for more than a few minutes - and yes, some obligatory song and dance routines that never quite work for me. Sure the roles of just about everyone have been exaggerated to maximise the polarity between good and evil, and I did struggle to take Stevenson's character seriously - artistically or historically; but it's those very excesses across the board that imbue this with a sense of excitement as it races along, admittedly quite predictably, to a showdown that's a bit light on the jeopardy front but is really quite entertaining to watch. Its a big screen experience, this, as much of the colour and vibrancy - even profligacy - of it's heavily CGI-reliant production will be lost on the telly, and with combat scenes galore throughout to support what is a very human story of freedom versus oppression, it's quite an impressive piece of cinema held together by two charismatic leading actors who look like they are enjoying themselves, too.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-24 08:28:42+03	2026-07-24 08:28:42+03
