11384	1769	96	1	Wait, what? Halloween? I guess Carpenter had the grandiose idea of expanding the franchise to one movie released around Halloween that was kind of about Halloween...the holiday not the movies.\r\n\r\nMike did die in Halloween II. It was a good idea, one year, one horror movie, all under the same anthology franchise.\r\n\r\nExcept the audience, after two stellar slasher films wanted Mike, so best laid plans of mice and men and all that.\r\n\r\nWhat we got instead of Mike was a movie that would have been a halfway decent stand alone end of the 70s horror film, one that was atmospheric, moody, and though it was too flawed to be big, it would have been a fair enough start to the franchise...it would have made a bit of money for the seasonal scare fair.\r\n\r\nExcept....it was called "Halloween III" and all the publicity in the world wouldn't have stopped the public from expecting Mike.\r\n\r\nSo, "robbed" would be the best phrase to describe how one feels whilst watching this film.\r\n\r\nEven now after knowing what it was intended to be...still feel a little robbed. And I am a fan of anthology series, I feel it lets writers and directors tell unique little stories that wouldn't otherwise be made.\r\n\r\nStill, Mike was a slasher and after Fred and Jason, you want to see him keep coming back until you vomit.	\N	\N	pending	2023-01-14 06:08:02+03	2023-01-14 06:08:02+03
11376	8906	11369	3	I recently obtained a large stack of blu-rays for cheap and this movie was among them.  I randomly chose this one to watch, as I’m a sucker for a good thriller.  The film was well shot, the acting good.  Lots of pretty people, and locations.  Much of the dialogue was hormonal teenager and frat boy and surface level deep.  The premise completely questionable, the plot rather ridiculous but believable given the set up, turned ridiculous as time went on - it still offered some twists and kept me guessing the whole time.  \r\n\r\nIn summary: this is five friends acting like self absorbed pigs and behaving badly and covering for each other.  The whole thing is a train wreck of characters who were tempting disaster.  A line that illustrates the biggest problems with this film comes from one of the leads to his brother, “we don’t treat women like that.” After their brother raped and beat a girl.  Which is so profoundly twisted coming from someone who is cheating on their wife (they all are) and then cover for his brother's crimes.  Somehow they write this in as if this line makes them on some higher moral ground.  \r\n\r\nDespite all of this, somehow I still could watch it even if goes against my standards. Mostly because I just wanted to know what happened and wondered if there would be some redemption for any one of these characters. Nope, it’s a modern thriller, that with a little more effort could have reworked the script to have someone who may have better motives and character instead we get lazy writing with very very very questionable men who every woman in the film, simply made the “mistake of falling for the wrong guy” and the guys still end up getting away with their crimes and behaviors and the women are just props and objects.  There is no depth to anyone in this film and that is too bad because the story actually had some potential to become something completely different.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-26 20:54:10+03	2026-07-27 00:12:10+03
