11921	7606	4356	5	For every **KILL BILL** fan out there around the world -- writer and director Quentin Tarantino has decreed that the *only* way to experience his original, uncut vision of **KILL BILL** (before a pre-incarcerated Harvey Weinstein's MIRAMAX convinced him to bifurcate the 4-hour revenge epic for monetary reasons) -- is right now in cinemas.\r\n\r\nThere is no plan to ever release the film on physical media in its current cinematic iteration as **THE** **WHOLE BLOODY AFFAIR**.\r\n\r\nIn other words, if you want to experience the four+ hour revenge epic -- now -- right NOW, the big screen is your only opportunity.\r\n\r\nWith some minor tweaks and extended scenes (especially the anime sequence originally from Vol.1, now gorier, of course) -- including a fully-colorized showdown with Thurman's Bride taking on the Crazy 88's in Tokyo -- also toned down in Vol. 1 from its original B&W rendering to mollify the MPAA who threatened to slap an NC-17 rating for violence -- little else seems altered.\r\n\r\nRe-experiencing Tarantino's fourth film becomes an ordeal with an ostensible intermission to give audiences a brief respite to come up for air from the veritable orgy of death.\r\n\r\nSeen in its full, gory glory -- by the time Thurman's Bride gets to David Carradine's Bill, we feel just as numb and exhausted with her quest for revenge as she is. But, of course, that is the point. I recently explained that the best way to watch HBO MAX's 15-episode **THE PITT** is to binge it in a single sitting for viewers to best expense the exhaustion of the characters -- something that works in similar ways with **THE WHOLE BLOODY AFFAIR**.\r\n\r\nUncut, unrated and untethered is probably the best way to experience Tarantino's blood-soaked KILL BILL -- and after twenty two years, it surprising how unparalleled it remains both for its epic scope, meticulous homages to the auteur's love of classic 1970s Kung Fu cinema, the samurai ethos', assassin philosophy and the films 