12079	3740	1315	1	The disappointment hits hard when a sequel ruins something you genuinely liked. While the original 1995 movie did a surprisingly good job bringing the arcade game to the big screen with dignity, this rushed follow-up completely dismantles that promising mythology. The first major hurdle is the abrupt recasting of almost the entire main cast. It immediately feels like watching a cheap TV pilot that couldn't afford its real stars, destroying the great group dynamic the first film built. Christopher Lambert’s magnetic, authoritative Raiden is replaced by James Remar, who is reduced to a passive exposition machine burdened with cheap family drama. Bridgette Wilson’s tough-as-nails Sonya Blade is swapped for Sandra Hess, whose character loses all her independence and spends most of her time just reacting in fear to bad CGI. But the ultimate insult is how they handled Johnny Cage. Because Linden Ashby didn't return, they brought in a stand-in just to kill off the franchise's best comic relief in the first five minutes. It’s a cheap, anticlimactic shock that completely kills the emotional stakes and sours the rest of the movie.\r\n\r\nOnly Robin Shou (Liu Kang) and Talisa Soto (Kitana) stick around to offer some familiarity, but Shou's genuine dedication to the martial arts gets buried under a terrible supporting cast. The villains only make things worse. Brian Thompson turns the famously imposing emperor Shao Kahn into a screaming, theatrical caricature, stripping away any sense of real danger. Behind the camera, losing director Paul W.S. Anderson was a fatal blow. John R. Leonetti takes over and delivers a technically awful movie that completely fails to capture the apocalyptic scale of an Earth invasion. The dark, mystical vibe of the first film is replaced by a cheap, daytime-TV aesthetic. Epic arenas are swapped out for empty warehouses, mud pits, and generic deserts, exposing the studio's desperate rush to cash in on the brand.\r\n\r\nThe script is just a flimsy excuse to stitch together fights, cramming in a bloated, disposable cast of characters from the third game—like Mileena, Ermac, and Rain—just to check off a marketing box. It ends up feeling like an empty parade of expensive cosplays where legendary fighters are reduced to generic henchmen. Even worse, the action itself is a mess. The grounded, visceral fights from the first movie are replaced by horrible wire-work. The actors float around in slow, gravity-defying stunts that completely lack the brutal impact you'd expect from Mortal Kombat. Add to that the laughably bad CGI, which looked outdated even for the 90s. The embarrassing final battle, where the characters turn into poorly rendered digital monsters, completely shatters whatever immersion is left.\r\n\r\nUltimately, Mortal Kombat: Annihilation proves that a rushed production can completely destroy a solid foundation by abandoning its visual identity, ruining the choreography, and delivering a chaotic script. Still, I recommend watching it just to form your own opinion. Some people genuinely find it entertaining in a "so bad it's good" kind of way, laughing at the technical flaws and enjoying the nostalgic energy of the nonstop techno soundtrack. If you manage your expectations, it’s actually a pretty fascinating exercise in seeing exactly how a big Hollywood action movie can miss the mark so spectacularly.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-13 05:59:45+03	2026-08-13 05:59:45+03
12083	12953	48	4	Before watching this film, I had absolutely no idea who Basil Brown (Ralph Fiennes), Edith Pretty (Carey Mulligan), or even Peggy Piggott (Lily James) were and what they did. As an engineer and a science enthusiast myself, I rarely struggle to find some sort of enjoyment in movies based on true stories about scientific discoveries, technological advancements, or impactful historical events. Therefore, despite a premise that doesn't immediately catch my attention, Simon Stone does a wonderful job in making this film quite interesting.\r\n\r\nMike Eley's cinematography produces some of the most gorgeous shots of the year, transforming The Dig into a visually satisfying movie to look at. The main narrative focuses on the excavation itself and the findings of a shockingly old (Anglo-Saxon) ship, which turns out to be more captivating than I ever anticipated. However, the romantic subplot involving Peggy Piggott feels out-of-place and rather dull considering the objective of this film. It really hurts the overall pacing, dragging an otherwise well-balanced flick.\r\n\r\nMoira Buffini's already excellent screenplay is still elevated by the remarkable cast. Fiennes (Harry Potter) and Mulligan (Promising Young Woman) share a heartwarming chemistry that really suits their characters. Lily James (Rebecca) also demonstrates her impressive emotional range once again. Overall, I recommend it to any viewer who's interested in knowing more about the real-life story or who's simply a fan of this specific genre.\r\n\r\nRating: B	\N	\N	pending	2021-07-15 19:22:29+03	2021-07-15 19:22:29+03
