12289	14501	1655	4	**Macabre anatomy of a fairy tale**\r\n\r\n"The Ugly Stepsister" stands out in its field because it successfully deconstructs a universal myth through striking imagery that directly engages with the aesthetic obsessions of our time. It is a unsettling film, a dark comedy, and a sharp satire inspired by the grim nature of the Brothers Grimm's "Aschenputtel." The movie leverages folklore to deliver a fierce critique of the modern fixation on looks, patriarchy, and imposed beauty standards. It has been a resounding success on the international festival circuit, even taking home the Best Motion Picture award at the prestigious Sitges Film Festival.\r\n\r\nIn a kingdom where beauty is the ultimate currency and social status is everything, Elvira travels with her sister Alma and her mother Rebecca toward the home of her new stepfather. There they meet his daughter, Agnes (a Cinderella embodied by the beautiful actress Thea Sofie Loch Næss, nicknamed 'Loch Ness' due to a joke by her witty parents), a young woman possessing a natural and ethereal beauty that perfectly fits the court's toxic standards.\r\n\r\nUnlike in classic versions, Agnes is no submissive victim; she is a woman fully aware of the power of her looks and her sexuality. Knowing that the grand royal ball and winning over Prince Julian are the only ways to secure her survival and power, she plays to win. In response, Elvira's mother pushes her daughter into a spiral of brutal competition, desperate to fit in and avoid rejection by the royals. To achieve this, Elvira resorts to macabre extremes of self-mutilation and primitive "cosmetic procedures" to alter her body and face, turning her own flesh into a bloody prison just to fit the slipper and win the prince's heart.\r\n\r\nEmilie Blichfeldt's debut is a cinematic exercise as fascinating as it is visceral. It builds a perfect bridge between the original gruesomeness of German folklore and the anxieties of 21st-century Western society. The film's strongest asset is its brilliant role reversal: by shifting the spotlight away from Cinderella and onto Elvira, the director does not seek a cheap redemption arc for "the villain," but rather to humanize her. Elvira is not inherently evil; she is a victim of her own mother and a social machine that tells her her body is not "good enough." The contrast built into the script is devastating: viewers will discover to their horror that, for the protagonist, the physical pain of the scalpel and blood is far more bearable than the emotional void of exclusion.\r\n\r\nThe production design recreates an opulent yet rotten courtly world, and the practical makeup effects are so realistic they turn your stomach. Blichfeldt handles dark humor with surgical precision, making the audience laugh at absurd situations only to instantly wipe the smile off their faces with scenes of explicit body horror that inherit the very best of genre cinema.	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-22 21:24:25+03	2026-08-22 21:24:25+03
