12544	12409	18	3	Set on a hot and humid afternoon in Chicago, this tells the tale of rising temperatures at a recording session by the eponymous blues singer (Viola Davis). She's already an huge success but is having difficulry asserting ownership over her own material and ensuring she gets paid properly for her music. Meantime, in another room her assembled backing musicians are increasingly treading on eggshells around each other as the waiting begins to pit them against each other. Most irritable of all seems to be the young "Levee" (Chadwick Boseman) who has ambitions of his own - both behind the microphone and with one of Ma's favourites (Taylour Paige), a woman who is quite happy to string him along. Trying to moderate his increasingly eccentric behaviour are "Toledo" (Glynn Turman) and "Cutler" (Colman Domingo) but as the afternoon rolls on, it appears that there is nobody in this room without demons of some sort or other. It's based on August Wilson's play and it really does look like it; indeed if you were to have filmed it as per one of those National Theatre live style of operations it might be hard to tell the difference later in the cinema. There are speeches, monologues if you like, peppering the drama but given it's about a musician there is a disappointing paucity of music. Davis is on convincing form, and I felt both Boseman and especially Turman delivered well too, but somehow I just didn't feel she really had that much to get her teeth into - save for a few scenes where attitude is queen and a couple of sassy numbers. Simply, there is just way too much chatter and not enough actual substance to sustain what I felt was actually quite a long and really quite dry ninety minutes, and at the end I felt that I'd have enjoyed it better on stage. It does look good, and the production designers have gone to meticulous lengths to recreate 1920s Illinois, but somehow this was all just a bit disjointed and lacking.	\N	\N	pending	2026-09-17 17:32:57+03	2026-09-17 17:32:57+03
12545	10538	1647	3	It feels like there's a really good movie hidden somewhere in here, but perhaps not a comedy.\r\n\r\nAs far as the comedy goes, its biggest obstacle is Brad Pitt. I remember seeing another comedy with him in it, and I remember him being not particularly good. This time around it's even worse, and it's clear to me that the man doesn't have a funny bone at all. \r\n\r\nThere's plenty of comedic material in the script, as delivered by the supporting cast, but to be frank, Brad Pitt is simply terrible. I don't remember having seen him this poor, ever, and I'm really quite a fan of him. Why he fails so terribly to be funny, I'm not sure. Perhaps he lacks the ability to do self-ridicule, perhaps he tries too hard, perhaps he doesn't really get humour in the first place, you be the judge — I just know he's not funny. At all. They really should have done better casting here. Lots of obvious candidates that would be better, and probably cheaper, too.\r\n\r\nThen there's the whole deal about it being a comedy in the first place. I don't consider myself a prune, but there are scenes in this film where the attempt at comedy just seems in awfully poor taste. Which is why I air the question of whether the "hidden good movie" in here should indeed be a comedy. The best scenes in War Machine most definitely aren't the funny ones, that's for sure.	\N	\N	pending	2020-11-02 20:58:20+03	2021-06-23 18:58:47+03
12556	12335	12549	4	A glimpse of the horrors faced by refugees escaping war-torn countries for a better life in Britain is provocatively probed in this stylish supernatural horror film from talented writer-director Remi Weekes. <a href="https://www.top10films.co.uk/67374-review-his-house-a-smart-chilling-contemporary-horror/">**His House**</a> unexpectedly blends the anxiety, confusion and alienation felt by a pair of asylum seekers from South Sudan with a nightmarish malevolence born from dark magic and folklore. [_Read my full review on <a href="https://www.top10films.co.uk/67374-review-his-house-a-smart-chilling-contemporary-horror/">Top 10 Films</a>_].	\N	\N	pending	2021-10-05 12:49:48+03	2021-10-26 00:33:29+03
12557	12335	18	3	When "Bol" (Sope Dirisu) and his wife "Rial" (Wunmi Mosaku) arrive in the UK after fleeing their war-torn home in South Sudan, they are given leave to remain whilst their case is further investigated, and housed in an ordinary - if a bit run down - housing estate. Helped by their case worker "Mark" (Matt Smith) they are given the basic essentials to get by, but as their stay becomes more extended they struggle to fit into a community that is at best disinterested, at worst a bit hostile - all whilst they start to believe that their new home is possessed! Things quite literally start going bump in the night and the couple begin to wonder if this has something to do with their ancestors, or perhaps the spirits of the folks they left behind to face the uncertainty. It's those last scenarios that we see expressed through dreams and flashbacks and that give us a clue as to just what horrors they escaped. Question is, though: are there now even greater horrors here for them now? At times this can be quite a compelling look at the psychology of people caught between two stools. Not fitting where they now are, not fitting where they once were. The cultural challenges they face now, though, aren't really developed and I found the characterisation of the ultra-tolerant "Mark" a little hard to believe, especially as the hunt for their ethereal nemesis starts to manifest itself in ways that become a little too surreal. Indeed, that's maybe the problem with this. It's trying to combine social and political points, but there's nowhere near enough punch from the dialogue and any message is bogged down in a confused hybrid of their supernatural experiences and the reality of their new British life. It can't have had much of a budget but that needn't have mattered had director Remi Weekes focussed more on keeping the story tight and on developing the characters a little more. It invites us to sympathise based on what he presumes are our own moral standards rather than present us with anything or anybody that's especially interesting. As ever, Smith completely underwhelms and though Mosaku offers us some emotional oomph, the rest of this is disappointingly unremarkable.	\N	\N	pending	2024-10-25 11:42:45+03	2024-10-25 11:42:45+03
12546	6194	393	3	For someone like me who has trouble visualizing what I read, this was helpful for me to imagine the book's setting. Other than that, there's not really much there. The movie took all the major plot points and put them one after the other, so I suppose it could be a more interesting alternative to Spark Notes, though I honestly think Spark Notes does a better job. It spent no time developing the characters and their relationships, thus failing to make me care about and understand them. There was also a severe lack of socio-political context, another large part of the characters' identities and actions.\r\n\r\nThis lackluster movie isn't an inherently bad reflection on the writer/director,\\* more a compliment to The Kite Runner author Khaled Hosseini's writing. So much of the book is internal monologue, providing little dialogue for a screenwriter to take directly, and each line feels important and symbolic. This is challenging to adapt for the screen. I feel that most would struggle similarly, and, all in all, I believe The Kite Runner is best left as a book.\r\n\r\nIf you haven't read The Kite Runner yet, please please please do! It deals with a lot of heavy themes, so I understand it's not for everyone, but, for me, the discomfort was worthwhile, as it's a truly amazing and impactful book. I found myself several times having to put the book down to let Hosseini's writing sit with me, and I don't think I'll ever forget the beauty and sadness of The Kite Runner.\r\n\r\n\\*I, for some reason, struggled greatly with finding a spot for this idea, so I'm putting it here. One choice I really loved was the decision to include Farsi. So many movies have characters of different nationalities speak English with their language's accent as an easy way out (ex: a Russian speaking to other Russians in English with a Russian accent). In The Kite Runner, however, any scene where Farsi would have been spoken is actually said in Farsi with English subtitles. I've never seen another movie do this consistently, only one or two scenes, and this choice made it feel more authentic.\r\n\r\n> (3/5, originally reviewed 2/24/25)	\N	\N	pending	2026-08-30 20:30:33+03	2026-08-31 00:50:46+03
