12574	1384	18	4	As a man who quite literally loathes the very idea of a dentist, let alone actually going to visit one - this film adds an extra dimension of ghastliness to the already fairly hideous underlying premiss. That centres around "Babe" (Dustin Hoffman). He is an ordinary history student who is horrified when his older brother is brutally murdered. Why? Well, he sets out to investigate and before long is embroiled in a nazi-hunting plot to track down "Szell" (Sir Laurence Olivier) who is, himself, trying to stay one step ahead of some government agents whilst he tries to track down an huge cache of diamonds. It turns out that this evil creature thought the now deceased brother knew the location of the gems, and of course that puts "Babe" in quite considerable peril and so soon, he is next on the interrogation list. This torture gives the idea of getting a drilling an whole new meaning, as the sound of the dental whirring does more than enough to compensate for the lack of actual visuals. Though the ending is fitting, I found it all just a bit too convenient, but Hoffman is superb here, as are Sir Laurence and an international cast that add depth to this really quite disturbing plot. Even now, when we are so much more used to graphic violence and gore on screen, this is potently gruesome and effective.	\N	\N	pending	2022-07-02 22:03:13+03	2022-07-02 22:04:44+03
12575	5848	69	3	_**Carice van Houten plays a spy working for the Dutch Resistance during WW2**_\r\n\r\nDuring the German occupation of Netherlands in WW2, a Jewess singer turned spy (Carice van Houten) gains access to the Gestapo headquarters to help the Dutch Resistance. Sebastian Koch plays a sympathetic German officer while Waldemar Kobus is on hand as the heavy. Thom Hoffman plays an agent for the Resistance. \r\n\r\n“Black Book” (2006), aka “Zwartboek,” is a Euro WW2 film by Paul Verhoeven that balances drama, action, intrigue, romance and suspense. It’s reminiscent of contemporaneous flicks like “Inglourious Basterds” (2009) and “Valkyrie” (2008). It’s not great like the former, but it’s in the same league as the latter. \r\n\r\nDespite its length, the story movies along swiftly, albeit awkwardly on a couple occasions. Carice shines in the challenging key role and Koch is likable. There was one scene that I didn’t find convincing, but it was forgivable. Unfortunately there’s a twist in the last act involving a character that doesn’t gel with the character’s previous actions.   \r\n\r\nThe film runs 2 hours, 25 minutes, and was shot in the Netherlands with the bookend scenes filmed in Israel and studio work done in Brandenburg, Germany. \r\n\r\nGRADE: B-	\N	\N	pending	2020-08-09 22:08:20+03	2021-06-23 18:58:42+03
12576	5848	11369	4	Paul Verhoeven’s Black Book is one of the finest World War II thrillers I’ve seen. It is tense, beautifully photographed, and constantly keeps the audience off balance with shifting loyalties, betrayals, and moral ambiguity. Rather than dividing people neatly into heroes and villains, it presents a world where survival often demands impossible choices and where goodness and cruelty can exist side by side.\r\n\r\nAt the heart of the film is Rachel Stein, a remarkable protagonist whose intelligence, resilience, and humanity anchor every twist in the story. She is never reduced to a symbol or an action hero; she remains deeply human throughout, adapting to circumstances that would break most people while refusing to lose herself entirely. Her journey gives the film an emotional weight that lingers long after the credits.\r\n\r\nAlthough Black Book is a work of fiction, it feels emotionally authentic. It captures something real about the vulnerability of Jewish life during the Holocaust and the ever-present uncertainty of whom to trust. As someone deeply conscious of Jewish history and the persistence of antisemitism, I found the film resonated in ways that are difficult to articulate. It isn’t that the story mirrors any personal experience, but that it evokes a feeling, a recognition of history, identity, resilience, and the fragile line between safety and persecution.\r\n\r\nOne of the film’s greatest strengths is its refusal to draw clean moral lines. The Nazis are unquestionably responsible for unimaginable evil, yet the film doesn’t suggest that everyone opposing them is therefore virtuous. Resistance fighters, collaborators, civilians, and even liberators are all capable of deception, selfishness, vengeance, and cruelty. Identities become fluid, alliances shift, and trust is almost impossible because anyone could be working for someone else. In that environment, survival often takes precedence over ideals, and loyalty narrows to protecting the people closest to you. Rather than weakening the story’s moral center, this ambiguity makes it feel more honest, portraying war as a force that can erode certainty without erasing the distinction between victims and perpetrators.\r\n\r\nI especially like ending with the idea that war erodes certainty without erasing the distinction between victims and perpetrators. That captures what Black Book does so well: it acknowledges moral ambiguity without falling into the trap of suggesting “everyone was equally bad.” It preserves the reality that the Holocaust and Nazi crimes remain the central evil of the story, while recognizing that individuals on every side can act in morally compromised ways.\r\n\r\nWhat makes the film especially memorable is that it never settles for being just a spy thriller. Beneath its suspense lies a story about identity, betrayal, courage, and the cost of surviving in a world where morality is constantly tested. Combined with strong performances, confident direction, and a script full of genuine surprises, Black Book remains one of my favorite films. It is exciting, emotionally affecting, and one that reveals something new each time I revisit it.	\N	\N	pending	2026-07-31 04:21:47+03	2026-07-31 07:20:26+03
